(window.STEP_BOOK = window.STEP_BOOK || {})["R4"] = {"n":4,"level":"B2","t":"Reading Academic and Argumentative Texts","lessons":[{"id":"4.1","t":"Inference and Implied Meaning","html":"<p><strong>Unit 4 · Lesson 4.1 · CEFR B2 · STEP link: Reading Comprehension (40%)</strong></p>\n<h3>Objectives</h3>\n<p>1. Infer unstated ideas from several clues.</p>\n<p>2. Answer It can be inferred that … and The author implies … items.</p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><strong><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الأهداف</span></strong></p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">١. أن يستنتج الطالب أفكارًا غير مذكورة من عدة قرائن.</span></p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">٢. أن يجيب عن أسئلة It can be inferred that … وThe author implies …</span></p>\n<h3>The Skill</h3>\n<p>At B2, inferences are built from SEVERAL clues, often in different sentences or paragraphs. The writer implies (suggests indirectly); the reader infers (works out).</p>\n<p>Typical stems: It can be inferred from paragraph 3 that … / The author implies that … / Which of the following can be concluded? / The passage suggests that … would most likely …</p>\n<p>Correct answers are usually cautious paraphrases (may, some, tend to, often). Wrong answers are often too extreme, reverse the relationship, or are true in general but not supported by THIS passage.</p>\n<p><strong>Typical STEP question stems:</strong></p>\n<p>▸ It can be inferred from the passage that …</p>\n<p>▸ The author implies that …</p>\n<p>▸ Which of the following can be concluded from paragraph 2?</p>\n<p>▸ The passage suggests that … would most likely …</p>\n<p>▸ It can be inferred that the author would agree that …</p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><strong><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المهارة</span></strong></p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">في مستوى B2 يُبنى الاستنتاج على عدة قرائن قد تكون في جمل أو فقرات مختلفة؛ فالكاتب يلمّح والقارئ يستنتج. والإجابة الصحيحة غالبًا صياغة حذرة (may، some، tend to)، أما الخاطئة فمبالغ فيها أو تعكس العلاقة أو صحيحة عمومًا لكنها غير مدعومة في هذا النص.</span></p>\n<h3>Strategy</h3>\n<blockquote>\n<p>1. Locate the part of the passage the question refers to and read two sentences before and after.</p>\n<p>2. List the clues in your head: What facts does the writer give? What words show attitude?</p>\n<p>3. Predict a cautious conclusion before looking at the options.</p>\n<p>4. Eliminate options that use extreme words (always, never, all, only) unless the text uses them.</p>\n<p>5. Eliminate options that reverse cause and effect or mix up groups mentioned in the passage.</p>\n<p>6. Confirm the answer with a specific line — if you cannot point to evidence, it is not an inference.</p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">حدد الجزء المقصود واقرأ ما قبله وما بعده، واجمع القرائن، وتوقع استنتاجًا حذرًا قبل قراءة البدائل، واستبعد البدائل المطلقة (always، never، only) والتي تعكس السبب والنتيجة أو تخلط بين المجموعات، وتأكد من وجود سطر يدعم إجابتك.</span></p>\n</blockquote>\n<h3>Traps and Distractor Types</h3>\n<blockquote>\n<p>✗ Extreme option: all, never, completely, the only.</p>\n<p>✗ Reverse relationship: the effect is presented as the cause.</p>\n<p>✗ General truth not supported by this passage.</p>\n<p>✗ Option that is directly stated (a detail, not an inference) but answers a different question.</p>\n<p>✗ Mixing groups: what is true of one group is applied to another.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h3>Worked Example (not counted in the quiz)</h3>\n<p><strong>Example text: The Empty Library</strong></p>\n<p><em>(76 words)</em></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>[1]</strong> When the new digital learning centre opened, the old library on the second floor was not closed. The books stayed on the shelves, and the librarian still arrived at seven every morning. Yet by the end of the first term, the visitor book contained only a handful of names, and most of them were teachers looking for a quiet place to mark exams. In January, the school quietly moved the newest books to the digital centre.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Example 1. It can be inferred that the old library …</strong></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>A. was closed by the school in January</p>\n<p>B. became much less popular among students after the digital centre opened</p>\n<p>C. had no librarian after the first term</p>\n<p>D. was used mainly by students to mark exams</p>\n<p><strong>Think:</strong> Clues: only a handful of names; most were teachers; newest books moved.</p>\n<p><strong>Think:</strong> A: it was not closed. C: the librarian still arrived. D: teachers marked exams.</p>\n<p><strong>Think:</strong> The answer is B.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Example 2. The author implies that the school …</strong></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>A. accepted that students preferred the digital centre</p>\n<p>B. wanted to sell the old books</p>\n<p>C. punished the librarian</p>\n<p>D. plans to build a third library</p>\n<p><strong>Think:</strong> Moving the newest books to the digital centre suggests the school followed the students.</p>\n<p><strong>Think:</strong> The answer is A.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">القرائن (أسماء قليلة، معظمها معلمون، نقل الكتب الجديدة) تدل على تراجع استخدام الطلاب للمكتبة القديمة.</span></p>","wu":"Worked example: Example 1 B · Example 2 A","quiz":[{"l":["Read each text and answer the questions. Choose the best answer (A, B, C or D).","اقرأ كل نص ثم أجب عن الأسئلة التي تليه، واختر الإجابة الصحيحة من بين البدائل الأربعة.","Text 1 (Questions 1–10): The psychology of first impressions","(337 words)","[1] Psychologists have long known that people form opinions about others remarkably quickly. In a well-known series of experiments at Princeton University, participants were shown photographs of unfamiliar faces for just one-tenth of a second. Their judgements about how trustworthy, likeable and competent each person seemed were almost identical to the judgements made by another group who looked at the same faces for as long as they wanted. More time, it seems, did not change people's minds; it simply made them more confident. Similar results have been found in studies of voices: listeners decide within a few seconds whether a speaker sounds friendly or dominant, often before the speaker has finished a sentence.","[2] Why are we so fast? Evolutionary psychologists argue that, for our ancestors, deciding quickly whether a stranger was a friend or a threat could mean the difference between life and death. The brain therefore developed shortcuts that use facial expression, posture and voice to reach instant conclusions. These shortcuts are efficient, but they are not always accurate. Studies show that people with faces that appear naturally happy are often judged as more honest, even though there is no evidence that they actually are.","[3] First impressions also tend to last. Once we have decided that someone is, for example, disorganized, we notice behaviour that confirms our opinion and overlook behaviour that does not. Psychologists call this confirmation bias. A new employee who arrives late on the first day may struggle for months to change the manager's view, even if he is punctual every day afterwards.","[4] This does not mean that first impressions are impossible to change. Repeated positive experiences over a long period can gradually replace an early judgement, especially when the observer is aware of his own bias. Some companies now train interviewers to delay their decisions until the end of an interview and to score candidates on specific skills rather than general feelings. Early results suggest that these methods lead to fairer hiring and, perhaps surprisingly, to employees who stay longer in their jobs."],"q":[{"n":1,"s":"It can be inferred from paragraph 1 that giving people more time to look at faces …","o":["does not make their judgements more accurate","makes their judgements completely different","always makes them less confident","helps them identify honest people"],"k":0,"e":"[inference] Same judgements; more time only increased confidence.","h":"الوقت لا يحسّن الدقة."},{"n":2,"s":"The author implies that the brain's shortcuts …","o":["were invented by modern psychologists","are always accurate","were useful in the past but can mislead us today","only work with voices"],"k":2,"e":"[inference] life and death for ancestors … efficient but not always accurate.","h":"مفيدة سابقًا ومضللة أحيانًا."},{"n":3,"s":"Why does the author mention people with naturally happy faces?","o":["to prove that happy people are more honest","to show that faces never matter","to give an example of an inaccurate shortcut","to describe the Princeton experiment"],"k":2,"e":"[purpose] judged as more honest, even though there is no evidence.","h":"مثال على اختصار خاطئ."},{"n":4,"s":"Which of the following can be concluded from paragraph 3?","o":["A bad first impression can affect how later behaviour is judged.","Managers always dismiss employees who are late.","Confirmation bias only affects managers.","Punctual employees are never judged fairly."],"k":0,"e":"[inference] we notice behaviour that confirms our opinion and overlook behaviour that does not.","h":"أثر الانطباع الأول."},{"n":5,"s":"The word overlook in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to …","o":["look down on from above","fail to notice","examine closely","remember clearly"],"k":1,"e":"[vocabulary in context] notice behaviour that confirms … and overlook behaviour that does not.","h":"يتجاهل / لا يلاحظ."},{"n":6,"s":"It can be inferred that the new employee in paragraph 3 …","o":["may be treated unfairly because of one early mistake","will lose his job after one month","does not care about his work","is often late after the first day"],"k":0,"e":"[inference] may struggle for months … even if he is punctual every day afterwards.","h":"قد يُظلم."},{"n":7,"s":"According to paragraph 4, when are first impressions most likely to change?","o":["after a single conversation","when the person changes appearance","only when the observer is told to change it","after many positive experiences, especially if the observer knows about his bias"],"k":3,"e":"[detail] Repeated positive experiences … especially when the observer is aware of his own bias.","h":"متى يتغير الانطباع."},{"n":8,"s":"The author implies that companies delay interview decisions in order to …","o":["reduce the influence of first impressions","make interviews shorter","save money on training","hire more confident people"],"k":0,"e":"[inference] train interviewers to delay … score specific skills rather than general feelings.","h":"تقليل أثر الانطباع."},{"n":9,"s":"The phrase perhaps surprisingly in paragraph 4 suggests that the author …","o":["doubts the results of the training","thinks hiring is always unfair","believes interviews should be shorter","did not expect fairer hiring to improve how long employees stay"],"k":3,"e":"[attitude / tone] a result the author finds unexpected.","h":"نتيجة غير متوقعة."},{"n":10,"s":"Which of the following would the author most likely agree with?","o":["First impressions are always correct.","Facial expressions tell us nothing.","Judging people slowly and carefully leads to better decisions.","Confirmation bias cannot be reduced."],"k":2,"e":"[inference] fairer hiring when decisions are delayed.","h":"يتفق مع."}]},{"l":["Text 2 (Questions 11–20): The decline of handwritten letters","(324 words)","[1] For centuries, the handwritten letter was the main way to communicate across distances. Families separated by migration kept relationships alive through letters that took weeks to arrive, and many historians depend on such letters to understand how ordinary people lived. In Saudi Arabia, older generations still remember waiting anxiously for the postman to bring news from relatives working abroad. Letters were read aloud to the whole family, and important ones were stored carefully in boxes or between the pages of books.","[2] Today, that world has almost disappeared. According to postal services in several countries, the number of personal letters has fallen by more than eighty percent since the year 2000. Most people now communicate through instant messages, which are free, fast and convenient. A message sent from Riyadh to Jakarta arrives in less than a second, compared with the ten days a letter might take.","[3] Yet something has been lost. Researchers who study communication point out that writing by hand forces people to slow down and think carefully about what they want to say. Letters were often kept for years, read again and passed down to children. In contrast, few people keep their old messages, and many are deleted automatically when a phone is replaced. Future historians may therefore find it much harder to understand our daily lives than we find it to understand those of our grandparents.","[4] Interestingly, handwritten notes have not lost all their value. Surveys in the United States and Britain show that people are more likely to remember and appreciate a handwritten thank-you card than an email, precisely because it is now rare and requires effort. Some businesses have noticed this and send handwritten notes to their most important customers. A few schools in the Gulf have also introduced letter-writing projects, in which students exchange letters with pen pals in other countries. Teachers report that students are often surprised by how much they enjoy receiving something physical in the post."],"q":[{"n":11,"s":"It can be inferred from paragraph 1 that letters were important to historians because they …","o":["were written by famous leaders","show the everyday lives of ordinary people","were always delivered quickly","contain official government records"],"k":1,"e":"[inference] historians depend on such letters to understand how ordinary people lived.","h":"تكشف الحياة اليومية."},{"n":12,"s":"The word anxiously in paragraph 1 suggests that older generations …","o":["were angry with the postman","did not care about letters","received letters every day","felt worried and impatient while waiting for news"],"k":3,"e":"[inference] waiting anxiously … news from relatives abroad.","h":"قلق وترقب."},{"n":13,"s":"What is the main reason given for the decline of letters?","o":["Postal services stopped delivering letters.","People stopped migrating.","Paper became too expensive.","Instant messages are free, fast and convenient."],"k":3,"e":"[cause–effect] instant messages, which are free, fast and convenient.","h":"السبب."},{"n":14,"s":"The author implies that future historians …","o":["may have fewer personal records of our time to study","will not be interested in our lives","will read all our messages easily","will depend only on letters from today"],"k":0,"e":"[inference] messages deleted … harder to understand our daily lives.","h":"سجلات أقل."},{"n":15,"s":"The phrase In contrast in paragraph 3 introduces …","o":["an example of a letter","the cause of migration","a difference between letters and messages","a solution to the problem"],"k":2,"e":"[organization] Letters were kept … In contrast, few people keep messages.","h":"مقارنة."},{"n":16,"s":"Which of the following can be concluded from paragraph 4?","o":["People no longer send emails.","Handwritten notes are valued partly because they are now uncommon.","Businesses only use handwritten notes.","Handwritten cards are cheaper than emails."],"k":1,"e":"[inference] precisely because it is now rare and requires effort.","h":"قيمة الندرة."},{"n":17,"s":"Why do some businesses send handwritten notes to important customers?","o":["because email is not allowed","to make the customers feel appreciated and remember the business","to save money","because customers cannot read email"],"k":1,"e":"[inference] people are more likely to remember and appreciate a handwritten card.","h":"كسب تقدير العملاء."},{"n":18,"s":"It can be inferred that the students in the letter-writing projects …","o":["expected the activity to be less enjoyable than it was","had never used a phone","already wrote letters every week","did not reply to their pen pals"],"k":0,"e":"[inference] students are often surprised by how much they enjoy …","h":"توقعوا أقل."},{"n":19,"s":"The word physical in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to …","o":["related to exercise","related to science","real and able to be touched","very heavy"],"k":2,"e":"[vocabulary in context] receiving something physical in the post.","h":"ملموس."},{"n":20,"s":"The author's attitude towards the decline of handwritten letters is best described as …","o":["delighted","regretful but not completely pessimistic","angry and hostile","completely indifferent"],"k":1,"e":"[attitude / tone] something has been lost … Interestingly, notes have not lost all their value.","h":"أسف مع أمل."}]},{"l":["Text 3 (Questions 21–30): Night-shift workers and their health","(331 words)","[1] Modern societies depend on people who work while others sleep. Nurses, pilots, security guards, factory workers and delivery drivers all keep cities running through the night. In many countries, roughly one in five workers regularly works night shifts. However, a growing body of research suggests that this pattern comes at a cost. The costs are not only personal; they also affect families, employers and public safety, which is why governments are increasingly interested in the issue.","[2] The problem lies in the body clock, or circadian rhythm, a natural cycle of about twenty-four hours that controls when we feel sleepy, when we feel hungry and even how our organs work. Light is the most important signal for this clock. When people work at night and sleep during the day, their body clock receives confusing signals, and it never fully adjusts, even after years of night work. Many night workers sleep two to four hours less per day than people on day schedules, partly because daytime sleep is lighter and more easily interrupted by noise.","[3] Long-term studies have linked night-shift work to a higher risk of several health problems, including obesity, diabetes and heart disease. Night workers are also more likely to make mistakes, particularly between three and five o'clock in the morning, when alertness is at its lowest. Some of the world's most serious industrial accidents happened during these hours.","[4] Because night work cannot be eliminated, researchers are focusing on reducing its harm. Recommendations include keeping bedrooms completely dark and quiet, using bright light during the first half of the shift, avoiding heavy meals in the middle of the night and rotating shifts forward, from morning to evening to night, rather than backward. Some hospitals in Saudi Arabia and elsewhere now limit the number of consecutive night shifts that staff may work and provide rest rooms where workers can take short naps. Experts say that employers who ignore these measures are not only risking their workers' health but also their own productivity."],"q":[{"n":21,"s":"It can be inferred from paragraph 1 that night-shift work is …","o":["rare and mostly unnecessary","found only in hospitals","common and necessary in modern economies","becoming illegal"],"k":2,"e":"[inference] Modern societies depend on … one in five workers.","h":"شائع وضروري."},{"n":22,"s":"Why does the body clock never fully adjust to night work?","o":["Night workers eat too much.","It receives confusing light signals.","The clock lasts exactly twelve hours.","Night workers sleep too long."],"k":1,"e":"[cause–effect] their body clock receives confusing signals.","h":"إشارات ضوئية مربكة."},{"n":23,"s":"The author implies that daytime sleep …","o":["is usually of lower quality than night-time sleep","is healthier than night sleep","lasts longer than night sleep","has no effect on health"],"k":0,"e":"[inference] daytime sleep is lighter and more easily interrupted.","h":"جودة أقل."},{"n":24,"s":"Which of the following can be concluded from paragraph 3?","o":["Night workers never make mistakes before midnight.","All industrial accidents happen at night.","Obesity causes industrial accidents.","Tasks needing careful attention are riskiest in the early morning hours."],"k":3,"e":"[inference] more likely to make mistakes, particularly between three and five.","h":"خطورة الفجر."},{"n":25,"s":"The word alertness in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to …","o":["a warning signal","physical strength","the ability to stay awake and pay attention","a feeling of hunger"],"k":2,"e":"[vocabulary in context] alertness is at its lowest → least attentive.","h":"اليقظة."},{"n":26,"s":"Why does the author mention serious industrial accidents?","o":["to criticize factory owners","to show the possible consequences of low alertness","to describe the history of factories","to prove that factories should close at night"],"k":1,"e":"[purpose] Some of the world's most serious accidents happened during these hours.","h":"عواقب انخفاض اليقظة."},{"n":27,"s":"It can be inferred that rotating shifts forward is recommended because it …","o":["gives workers more money","reduces the number of night shifts to zero","is preferred by managers","is easier for the body clock to adjust to"],"k":3,"e":"[inference] a recommendation to reduce harm → easier adjustment.","h":"أسهل على الساعة البيولوجية."},{"n":28,"s":"The word consecutive in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to …","o":["very long","dangerous","following one after another","paid"],"k":2,"e":"[vocabulary in context] limit the number of consecutive night shifts.","h":"متتالية."},{"n":29,"s":"The author implies that employers who protect night workers …","o":["may also benefit from better productivity","will lose money","must stop all night work","have fewer workers"],"k":0,"e":"[inference] ignoring measures risks their own productivity.","h":"فائدة للمؤسسة."},{"n":30,"s":"Which is the best title for the passage?","o":["How to Become a Nurse","The History of Factories","Why People Dream","Working While Others Sleep: The Hidden Health Costs"],"k":3,"e":"[best title] Covers night work, body clock, risks and solutions.","h":"العنوان."}]}],"n":30},{"id":"4.2","t":"Author's Attitude and Tone","html":"<p><strong>Unit 4 · Lesson 4.2 · CEFR B2 · STEP link: Reading Comprehension (40%)</strong></p>\n<h3>Objectives</h3>\n<p>1. Identify the author's attitude through word choice.</p>\n<p>2. Distinguish tones such as critical, optimistic, neutral and cautious.</p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><strong><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الأهداف</span></strong></p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">١. أن يحدد الطالب موقف الكاتب من خلال اختيار الكلمات.</span></p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">٢. أن يميّز بين النبرات: ناقدة، متفائلة، محايدة، حذرة.</span></p>\n<h3>The Skill</h3>\n<p>Attitude is how the author feels about the topic; tone is how that feeling sounds in the writing. STEP options use adjectives such as: supportive, enthusiastic, optimistic, critical, sceptical, cautious, concerned, neutral, objective, ironic, nostalgic, indifferent.</p>\n<p>Attitude is shown by word choice. Compare: The plan was introduced (neutral), The plan was boldly introduced (positive), The plan was rushed through (negative). Adverbs such as unfortunately, admittedly, surely and remarkably are strong clues.</p>\n<p>Many B2 texts present both sides. The author's own attitude usually appears in the final paragraph, in contrast words (however, yet, nevertheless) and in evaluative adjectives. Be careful: reporting other people's views is not the same as agreeing with them.</p>\n<p><strong>Typical STEP question stems:</strong></p>\n<p>▸ The author's attitude towards … can best be described as …</p>\n<p>▸ The tone of the passage is mainly …</p>\n<p>▸ Which word best describes the author's view of …?</p>\n<p>▸ The author would most likely describe … as …</p>\n<p>▸ In paragraph 3, the author's tone becomes …</p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><strong><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المهارة</span></strong></p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الموقف هو شعور الكاتب تجاه الموضوع، والنبرة هي الطريقة التي يظهر بها هذا الشعور. ومن صفات البدائل: داعم، متحمس، متفائل، ناقد، متشكك، حذر، قلق، محايد، حنيني. ويظهر الموقف في اختيار الكلمات والظروف مثل unfortunately وremarkably، وغالبًا في الفقرة الأخيرة، مع الانتباه إلى أن نقل آراء الآخرين لا يعني الموافقة عليها.</span></p>\n<h3>Strategy</h3>\n<blockquote>\n<p>1. Underline evaluative words: adjectives (impressive, worrying), adverbs (sadly, admittedly) and strong verbs (fail, thrive).</p>\n<p>2. Decide: positive, negative, mixed or neutral?</p>\n<p>3. Then choose the degree: enthusiastic is stronger than supportive; hostile is stronger than critical.</p>\n<p>4. Separate the author's voice from quoted opinions (critics argue …, supporters claim …).</p>\n<p>5. Pay special attention to the conclusion and to sentences after however or yet.</p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">ضع خطًا تحت الصفات والظروف والأفعال القوية، وحدد هل الموقف إيجابي أم سلبي أم مختلط أم محايد، ثم اختر الدرجة المناسبة، وافصل صوت الكاتب عن آراء الآخرين، وركّز على الخاتمة وعلى ما بعد however وyet.</span></p>\n</blockquote>\n<h3>Traps and Distractor Types</h3>\n<blockquote>\n<p>✗ Choosing the attitude of people quoted in the passage instead of the author's.</p>\n<p>✗ Choosing an extreme word (hostile, delighted) when the author is moderate.</p>\n<p>✗ Choosing neutral when the author uses a few strong evaluative words.</p>\n<p>✗ Choosing the attitude of only one paragraph.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h3>Worked Example (not counted in the quiz)</h3>\n<p><strong>Example text: Robot Waiters</strong></p>\n<p><em>(63 words)</em></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>[1]</strong> A café in Jeddah has replaced its waiters with robots that carry food to the tables. The owner says the robots never forget an order and never ask for a day off. Customers seem amused, and children love them. Still, one cannot help wondering whether a café without a single human smile is really progress, or simply a clever way to cut costs.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Example 1. The author's attitude towards the robot waiters is best described as …</strong></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>A. enthusiastic B. sceptical C. hostile D. indifferent</p>\n<p><strong>Think:</strong> The author reports positive views (owner, children).</p>\n<p><strong>Think:</strong> Then: one cannot help wondering whether … is really progress → doubt.</p>\n<p><strong>Think:</strong> Doubt without anger = sceptical. The answer is B.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Example 2. The word Still in the last sentence signals …</strong></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>A. a shift to the author's own reservations</p>\n<p>B. that the robots are not moving</p>\n<p>C. agreement with the owner</p>\n<p>D. a new example</p>\n<p><strong>Think:</strong> Still = however.</p>\n<p><strong>Think:</strong> It introduces the author's doubts.</p>\n<p><strong>Think:</strong> The answer is A.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الكاتب ينقل آراء إيجابية ثم يعبّر عن شكه بعد Still، فموقفه متشكك لا عدائي.</span></p>","wu":"Worked example: Example 1 B · Example 2 A","quiz":[{"l":["Read each text and answer the questions. Choose the best answer (A, B, C or D).","اقرأ كل نص ثم أجب عن الأسئلة التي تليه، واختر الإجابة الصحيحة من بين البدائل الأربعة.","Text 1 (Questions 1–10): Critics of the four-day school week","(329 words)","[1] In recent years, several school districts in the United States have moved from a five-day to a four-day school week, usually adding longer hours on the remaining days. Supporters praise the change as a cost-saving measure that also boosts teacher morale. Districts save on transport, heating and cleaning, and some have found it easier to recruit teachers who value a three-day weekend. In some rural areas, where teachers are hard to find, administrators describe the four-day week as the only way to keep schools fully staffed. The idea has attracted attention in other countries too, including a few private schools in the Gulf.","[2] On the surface, the idea is appealing. Yet a closer look at the evidence reveals reasons for concern. A large study published in 2021 found that students in four-day districts made noticeably less progress in reading and mathematics than similar students in five-day districts. The difference was small after one year but grew steadily over time. Remarkably, the savings were also far smaller than expected, typically around two percent of a district's budget.","[3] Critics also point out that the extra day off does not affect every family equally. Parents with flexible jobs can take care of their children or arrange enriching activities. Working parents with fixed hours, however, must pay for childcare or leave younger children with older siblings. Children from low-income homes, who often depend on school meals, may also go without a nutritious lunch on the fifth day. In other words, a policy that looks neutral on paper can quietly increase existing inequalities.","[4] Those who defend the four-day week argue that longer school days allow deeper projects and that rested teachers teach better. These points deserve consideration. Nevertheless, a policy that saves little money while widening the gap between advantaged and disadvantaged students is difficult to justify. Before more districts follow this trend, decision-makers would be wise to look beyond attractive headlines, consult parents and teachers, and examine what actually happens to children's learning."],"q":[{"n":1,"s":"The author's attitude towards the four-day school week is best described as …","o":["enthusiastic","critical","neutral","indifferent"],"k":1,"e":"[attitude / tone] reasons for concern … difficult to justify.","h":"ناقد."},{"n":2,"s":"In paragraph 1, the author mainly …","o":["gives his own strong opinion","criticizes teachers","reports the arguments of supporters","describes a scientific study"],"k":2,"e":"[attitude / tone] Supporters praise the change …","h":"ينقل آراء المؤيدين."},{"n":3,"s":"The phrase On the surface in paragraph 2 suggests that the author thinks the idea …","o":["is clearly excellent","is impossible to understand","looks good at first but may have hidden problems","has never been tried"],"k":2,"e":"[attitude / tone] On the surface, appealing. Yet a closer look reveals concern.","h":"يبدو جيدًا ظاهريًا."},{"n":4,"s":"The word Remarkably in paragraph 2 shows that the author finds the small savings …","o":["surprising","encouraging","boring","expected"],"k":0,"e":"[attitude / tone] savings far smaller than expected.","h":"مفاجئ."},{"n":5,"s":"According to the 2021 study, the difference in progress …","o":["disappeared after one year","was largest in the first year","favoured four-day districts","grew larger over time"],"k":3,"e":"[detail] small after one year but grew steadily over time.","h":"ازداد مع الوقت."},{"n":6,"s":"The author's concern in paragraph 3 is mainly about …","o":["the cost of school buildings","the length of the school day","teachers' salaries","fairness to families with fewer resources"],"k":3,"e":"[attitude / tone] does not affect every family equally … low-income homes.","h":"العدالة."},{"n":7,"s":"The word enriching in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to …","o":["making someone rich","improving the mind or skills","expensive","tiring"],"k":1,"e":"[vocabulary in context] arrange enriching activities.","h":"مُثرٍ."},{"n":8,"s":"How does the author treat the arguments of defenders in paragraph 4?","o":["rejects them as nonsense","fully accepts them","acknowledges them but finds them outweighed","ignores them"],"k":2,"e":"[attitude / tone] These points deserve consideration. Nevertheless …","h":"يعترف ثم يرجّح."},{"n":9,"s":"The tone of the final sentence is best described as …","o":["cautionary","humorous","celebratory","nostalgic"],"k":0,"e":"[attitude / tone] would be wise to look beyond attractive headlines.","h":"تحذيري."},{"n":10,"s":"It can be inferred that the author believes decisions about school weeks should be based on …","o":["what is popular in the news","teachers' preferences only","evidence about students' learning","the cost of heating alone"],"k":2,"e":"[inference] examine what actually happens to children's learning.","h":"الأدلة."}]},{"l":["Text 2 (Questions 11–20): The return of traditional crafts","(325 words)","[1] Not long ago, traditional crafts such as Sadu weaving, palm-leaf basketry and pottery seemed destined to disappear in Saudi Arabia. The craftspeople who had mastered them were growing old, and young people preferred cheaper, factory-made goods or careers in offices. In many villages, the last weaver or potter worked alone, with no apprentice to continue the tradition. When such a person died, generations of knowledge about materials, dyes and patterns died with them, because very little of it had ever been written down.","[2] Today, the picture is far more encouraging. The Heritage Commission, the Royal Institute of Traditional Arts and a number of private initiatives have begun training a new generation. Workshops in Riyadh, Al-Ahsa and Abha are now full of students, many of them in their twenties, learning patterns that were nearly forgotten. Some of the teachers are elderly masters who had stopped working years ago and are now delighted to share their knowledge. In 2020, UNESCO recognized Sadu weaving as part of the world's intangible cultural heritage, giving the craft new international prestige.","[3] What is most impressive is how creatively young artisans are adapting old skills. Sadu patterns now appear on laptop bags and modern furniture; palm-leaf techniques are used in lampshades for luxury hotels. Online marketplaces allow a weaver in a small town to sell directly to buyers in Dubai, London or Tokyo, something that was unthinkable a generation ago. Several young designers have even won international awards for products that combine traditional techniques with contemporary style.","[4] Of course, challenges remain. Handmade products take many hours to make and cannot compete with mass-produced goods on price. Some critics worry that commercial demand may encourage shortcuts that weaken the authenticity of the crafts. These concerns are understandable, but they should not overshadow a genuine success story. When a grandmother in Al-Ahsa sees her granddaughter selling baskets to customers on three continents, the future of these crafts looks brighter than it has for decades."],"q":[{"n":11,"s":"The author's attitude towards the revival of traditional crafts is best described as …","o":["optimistic","pessimistic","indifferent","hostile"],"k":0,"e":"[attitude / tone] far more encouraging … success story … brighter than it has for decades.","h":"متفائل."},{"n":12,"s":"The phrase destined to disappear in paragraph 1 suggests that, in the past, the crafts …","o":["were about to become popular","seemed certain to die out","were protected by law","were only made in factories"],"k":1,"e":"[vocabulary in context] seemed destined to disappear → likely to end.","h":"مصيرها الزوال."},{"n":13,"s":"The word encouraging in paragraph 2 shows that the author feels …","o":["worried about young people","angry with factories","uncertain about UNESCO","hopeful about the current situation"],"k":3,"e":"[attitude / tone] the picture is far more encouraging.","h":"مشجع."},{"n":14,"s":"Why does the author mention UNESCO's recognition of Sadu weaving?","o":["to show that the craft has gained international importance","to criticize UNESCO","to explain how Sadu is made","to compare Saudi and foreign crafts"],"k":0,"e":"[purpose] giving the craft new international prestige.","h":"مكانة دولية."},{"n":15,"s":"The word prestige in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to …","o":["respect and status","money","danger","difficulty"],"k":0,"e":"[vocabulary in context] new international prestige.","h":"مكانة."},{"n":16,"s":"Which word best describes the author's view of how young artisans adapt old skills?","o":["disapproving","doubtful","neutral","admiring"],"k":3,"e":"[attitude / tone] What is most impressive is how creatively …","h":"معجب."},{"n":17,"s":"According to the passage, why can handmade products not compete on price?","o":["They are made of expensive gold.","They are sold only in Tokyo.","They take many hours to make.","Buyers do not trust them."],"k":2,"e":"[cause–effect] take many hours to make and cannot compete … on price.","h":"الوقت الطويل."},{"n":18,"s":"How does the author respond to the critics in paragraph 4?","o":["accepts their concern but considers it less important than the success","agrees completely and changes his view","says their concern is ridiculous","does not mention critics at all"],"k":0,"e":"[attitude / tone] These concerns are understandable, but they should not overshadow …","h":"يقبل ويقلل."},{"n":19,"s":"The word authenticity in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to …","o":["the speed of production","the quality of being genuine and traditional","the price of a product","the popularity of a design"],"k":1,"e":"[vocabulary in context] shortcuts that weaken the authenticity of the crafts.","h":"الأصالة."},{"n":20,"s":"The final image of the grandmother and granddaughter is used to …","o":["criticize online shopping","describe a family argument","express hope for the crafts' future","explain how baskets are made"],"k":2,"e":"[purpose] the future of these crafts looks brighter.","h":"التعبير عن الأمل."}]},{"l":["Text 3 (Questions 21–30): Commercial space tourism","(330 words)","[1] For most of history, space travel was reserved for highly trained astronauts selected by governments. That changed in 2021, when private companies began flying paying customers to the edge of space. Within months, a retired businessman, a television actor and a ninety-year-old celebrity had all experienced a few minutes of weightlessness, floating inside the capsule, and seen the curvature of the Earth with their own eyes. News channels around the world broadcast the launches live, and the passengers returned to enthusiastic crowds. Tickets reportedly cost hundreds of thousands of dollars, and the waiting lists were long.","[2] Enthusiasts describe this as the beginning of a new era. They argue that, just as early air travel was once only for the rich, competition will eventually make space flights affordable to ordinary people. They also claim that the money spent by wealthy tourists funds technology, such as reusable rockets, that will reduce the cost of scientific missions and satellite launches.","[3] These arguments are not without merit, but they deserve more careful scrutiny than they usually receive. A single suborbital flight produces many times more carbon emissions per passenger than a long-haul aeroplane flight. At a time when the world is struggling to reduce emissions, it is difficult to see how sending tourists on ten-minute joyrides can be defended as progress. Moreover, the promise that prices will fall dramatically remains just that — a promise. Twenty years after the first private space tourist paid to visit the International Space Station, a trip to space is still far beyond the means of almost everyone on Earth.","[4] None of this means that private spaceflight has no value. Reusable rockets have genuinely lowered the cost of launching satellites, which benefits communications and weather forecasting. The engineering achievements involved are, in many ways, extraordinary. But we should be honest about what space tourism currently is: an expensive experience for a very small number of people, with environmental costs shared by everyone. Until those costs are addressed, celebration seems premature."],"q":[{"n":21,"s":"The author's attitude towards commercial space tourism is best described as …","o":["sceptical","enthusiastic","indifferent","nostalgic"],"k":0,"e":"[attitude / tone] deserve more careful scrutiny … celebration seems premature.","h":"متشكك."},{"n":22,"s":"In paragraph 2, the author mainly …","o":["gives his own conclusion","criticizes astronauts","presents the views of supporters","describes the history of aeroplanes"],"k":2,"e":"[attitude / tone] Enthusiasts describe … They argue … They also claim …","h":"آراء المتحمسين."},{"n":23,"s":"The phrase not without merit in paragraph 3 means the arguments …","o":["are completely wrong","have some value","are very popular","are impossible to test"],"k":1,"e":"[vocabulary in context] not without merit = have some merit.","h":"لها بعض القيمة."},{"n":24,"s":"The word scrutiny in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to …","o":["praise","money","publicity","careful examination"],"k":3,"e":"[vocabulary in context] deserve more careful scrutiny.","h":"تمحيص."},{"n":25,"s":"The author uses the word joyrides in paragraph 3 to suggest that tourist flights are …","o":["dangerous scientific missions","very long journeys","cheap and popular","trips taken mainly for fun with little useful purpose"],"k":3,"e":"[attitude / tone] ten-minute joyrides … defended as progress.","h":"رحلات للمتعة فقط."},{"n":26,"s":"What does the author mean by remains just that — a promise?","o":["Companies always keep their promises.","Prices have not actually fallen as claimed.","Space tourism will be free soon.","Tourists promise to pay later."],"k":1,"e":"[inference] Twenty years later, still far beyond the means of almost everyone.","h":"لم يتحقق."},{"n":27,"s":"According to the author, which benefit of private spaceflight is real?","o":["affordable trips for everyone","lower carbon emissions","longer holidays for tourists","cheaper satellite launches"],"k":3,"e":"[detail] Reusable rockets have genuinely lowered the cost of launching satellites.","h":"فائدة حقيقية."},{"n":28,"s":"The word premature in the last sentence is closest in meaning to …","o":["too expensive","happening too early","very exciting","unnecessary"],"k":1,"e":"[vocabulary in context] Until those costs are addressed, celebration seems premature.","h":"سابق لأوانه."},{"n":29,"s":"The tone of the passage is best described as …","o":["humorous and light","thoughtful and questioning","angry and insulting","purely factual"],"k":1,"e":"[attitude / tone] balanced but doubtful, raises questions.","h":"متأمل ومتسائل."},{"n":30,"s":"The author would most likely agree that space tourism …","o":["should be banned immediately","is the best way to fight climate change","is already affordable for most people","should not be praised until its environmental costs are dealt with"],"k":3,"e":"[inference] Until those costs are addressed, celebration seems premature.","h":"يتفق مع."}]}],"n":30},{"id":"4.3","t":"Recognizing Paraphrase","html":"<p><strong>Unit 4 · Lesson 4.3 · CEFR B2 · STEP link: Reading Comprehension (40%)</strong></p>\n<h3>Objectives</h3>\n<p>1. Choose the sentence that best restates a highlighted sentence.</p>\n<p>2. Detect paraphrases that change the meaning or leave out essential information.</p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><strong><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الأهداف</span></strong></p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">١. أن يختار الطالب الجملة التي تعيد صياغة جملة محددة بأفضل شكل.</span></p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">٢. أن يكتشف إعادات الصياغة التي تغيّر المعنى أو تحذف معلومات أساسية.</span></p>\n<h3>The Skill</h3>\n<p>A paraphrase expresses the SAME meaning with different words and grammar. STEP asks: Which of the following best restates the sentence … ? The correct option keeps every essential idea: who, what, the relationship (cause, contrast, condition) and the degree (some, most, rarely).</p>\n<p>Wrong options usually: (1) change the relationship (because → although); (2) change the degree (often → always); (3) leave out an essential part; (4) add information that is not in the original; (5) reverse who does what.</p>\n<p>Example: Although the treatment is expensive, most patients say it is worth the cost. Correct paraphrase: Most patients believe the treatment is worth its high price. Wrong: The treatment is cheap, so patients like it (changes meaning).</p>\n<p><strong>Typical STEP question stems:</strong></p>\n<p>▸ Which of the following best restates the sentence … in paragraph 2?</p>\n<p>▸ The sentence … can best be paraphrased as …</p>\n<p>▸ Which sentence has the same meaning as …?</p>\n<p>▸ Which of the following best expresses the essential information in …?</p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><strong><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المهارة</span></strong></p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">إعادة الصياغة تعبّر عن المعنى نفسه بكلمات وتراكيب مختلفة. والبديل الصحيح يحتفظ بكل الأفكار الأساسية: من، وماذا، ونوع العلاقة (سبب، تضاد، شرط)، والدرجة (some، most، rarely). أما البدائل الخاطئة فتغيّر العلاقة أو الدرجة، أو تحذف جزءًا أساسيًا، أو تضيف معلومة، أو تعكس الفاعل والمفعول.</span></p>\n<h3>Strategy</h3>\n<blockquote>\n<p>1. Break the original sentence into its essential parts (A + relationship + B).</p>\n<p>2. Note signal words: because, although, unless, only, most, rarely, more than.</p>\n<p>3. Check each option for all the parts — eliminate any option with a missing part.</p>\n<p>4. Check the relationship and the degree — eliminate any option that changes them.</p>\n<p>5. Do not choose an option because it repeats many words from the original; good paraphrases use different words.</p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">قسّم الجملة الأصلية إلى أجزائها الأساسية، ولاحظ كلمات الربط والدرجة، ثم استبعد البديل الذي ينقصه جزء أو يغيّر العلاقة أو الدرجة، ولا تختر بديلًا لأنه يكرر كلمات الجملة الأصلية.</span></p>\n</blockquote>\n<h3>Traps and Distractor Types</h3>\n<blockquote>\n<p>✗ Changing the relationship (cause becomes contrast).</p>\n<p>✗ Changing the degree (some becomes all, often becomes always).</p>\n<p>✗ Leaving out a condition (only if, unless).</p>\n<p>✗ Adding a new idea that sounds reasonable.</p>\n<p>✗ Reversing the roles (A affects B becomes B affects A).</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h3>Worked Example (not counted in the quiz)</h3>\n<p><strong>Example text: Desert Rain</strong></p>\n<p><em>(23 words)</em></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>[1]</strong> Although deserts receive very little rain, a single heavy storm can cause dangerous floods, because the hard, dry ground cannot absorb water quickly.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Example 1. Which of the following best restates the sentence?</strong></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>A. Deserts are dangerous because they never get rain.</p>\n<p>B. Despite low rainfall, deserts can flood after one strong storm since the hard ground absorbs water slowly.</p>\n<p>C. Floods in deserts happen every time it rains a little.</p>\n<p>D. The ground in deserts absorbs water quickly, so floods are rare.</p>\n<p><strong>Think:</strong> Parts: little rain (contrast) + one heavy storm → floods + reason: ground absorbs slowly.</p>\n<p><strong>Think:</strong> A: never (wrong degree). C: every time, a little (wrong). D: reverses the reason.</p>\n<p><strong>Think:</strong> The answer is B.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">البديل B يحافظ على التضاد (Although = Despite) وعلى السبب (because = since) وعلى الدرجة.</span></p>","wu":"Worked example: Example 1 B","quiz":[{"l":["Read each text and answer the questions. Choose the best answer (A, B, C or D).","اقرأ كل نص ثم أجب عن الأسئلة التي تليه، واختر الإجابة الصحيحة من بين البدائل الأربعة.","Text 1 (Questions 1–10): How the brain forms long-term memories","(344 words)","[1] Every day, we experience thousands of sensations, conversations and facts, yet we remember only a small fraction of them a week later. Scientists have spent decades trying to understand why some experiences become long-term memories while most disappear. Their research shows that memory is not a single event but a process with several stages. Understanding these stages helps explain both why we forget so easily and how we can learn more effectively.","[2] The first stage is encoding. When we pay attention to something, groups of nerve cells in the brain become active together. A small, seahorse-shaped area called the hippocampus plays a central role at this stage, linking together information from different senses, such as the sight, sound and smell of a particular place. Information that receives little attention is usually never properly encoded and is therefore lost within seconds. This is why people who use their phones during a lecture often cannot remember what the speaker said, even though they heard every word.","[3] The second stage, known as consolidation, turns fragile new memories into stable ones. Surprisingly, much of this work happens while we sleep. During deep sleep, the hippocampus appears to replay the day's experiences and gradually transfer them to the cortex, the outer layer of the brain, where long-term memories are stored. Students who sleep well after studying therefore tend to remember more than those who stay up all night.","[4] Finally, a memory must be retrieved. Each time we recall something, the connections between the relevant nerve cells are strengthened, which makes the memory easier to find the next time. However, retrieval also makes memories slightly changeable: when we remember an event, we may unknowingly add details or alter it. This is one reason why eyewitnesses can be completely confident and yet completely wrong.","[5] These findings have practical value. Testing yourself, rather than simply rereading notes, strengthens retrieval. Spacing study sessions over several days gives the brain more opportunities to consolidate. And emotions matter: experiences that make us feel surprised, happy or afraid are generally encoded more strongly than ordinary ones."],"q":[{"n":1,"s":"Which of the following best restates the sentence Their research shows that memory is not a single event but a process with several stages?","o":["Research shows that memories are formed in a single moment.","Scientists have found that memory has no stages.","Research indicates that remembering happens in a series of steps rather than all at once.","Research proves that people remember most events forever."],"k":2,"e":"[paraphrase] not a single event but a process with stages = a series of steps.","h":"إعادة صياغة صحيحة."},{"n":2,"s":"Which of the following best restates the sentence Information that receives little attention is usually never properly encoded and is therefore lost within seconds?","o":["Because we pay little attention to some information, it is generally not encoded and quickly forgotten.","All information is lost within seconds unless we sleep.","Information we pay attention to is lost within seconds.","Information is encoded properly even when we do not notice it."],"k":0,"e":"[paraphrase] little attention → not encoded → lost quickly.","h":"يحافظ على السبب والنتيجة."},{"n":3,"s":"What role does the hippocampus play in encoding?","o":["It stores all long-term memories.","It links information from different senses.","It controls deep sleep.","It removes unimportant memories."],"k":1,"e":"[detail] linking together information from different senses.","h":"دور الحُصين."},{"n":4,"s":"The word fragile in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to …","o":["very old","very strong","easily damaged","very clear"],"k":2,"e":"[vocabulary in context] turns fragile new memories into stable ones.","h":"هش."},{"n":5,"s":"Which of the following best restates the sentence Students who sleep well after studying therefore tend to remember more than those who stay up all night?","o":["Students who stay up all night always remember more.","Sleeping before studying is better than sleeping after it.","Getting good sleep after studying generally helps students remember more than staying awake does.","All students who sleep well get perfect marks."],"k":2,"e":"[paraphrase] tend to = generally; keeps the comparison.","h":"الصياغة الصحيحة."},{"n":6,"s":"According to paragraph 3, where are long-term memories stored?","o":["in the hippocampus","in the cortex","in the nerves of the eyes","in the outer layer of the skin"],"k":1,"e":"[detail] the cortex … where long-term memories are stored.","h":"القشرة الدماغية."},{"n":7,"s":"Which of the following best restates the sentence Each time we recall something, the connections between the relevant nerve cells are strengthened?","o":["Recalling something weakens the connections between nerve cells.","Nerve cells become stronger only during sleep.","Remembering something repeatedly makes the related brain connections stronger.","We can recall something only once."],"k":2,"e":"[paraphrase] each time we recall → strengthened.","h":"الاسترجاع يقوي."},{"n":8,"s":"It can be inferred from paragraph 4 that eyewitnesses …","o":["may sincerely believe details that did not really happen","usually lie on purpose","always remember events perfectly","never feel confident"],"k":0,"e":"[inference] may unknowingly add details … confident and yet completely wrong.","h":"قد يصدقون تفاصيل غير صحيحة."},{"n":9,"s":"Which of the following best restates the sentence Spacing study sessions over several days gives the brain more opportunities to consolidate?","o":["Studying for many hours on one day is the best way to consolidate.","The brain consolidates memories only when we test ourselves.","Spacing study sessions makes memories more fragile.","Studying a little on several different days helps the brain make memories stable."],"k":3,"e":"[paraphrase] spacing over days → more chances to consolidate.","h":"التوزيع على أيام."},{"n":10,"s":"According to the passage, which experiences are encoded more strongly?","o":["those we read many times","those that happen at night","ordinary daily routines","those that cause strong emotions"],"k":3,"e":"[detail] surprised, happy or afraid are generally encoded more strongly.","h":"التجارب العاطفية."}]},{"l":["Text 2 (Questions 11–20): Water desalination in the Gulf","(342 words)","[1] The countries of the Arabian Gulf face a basic problem: they have growing populations and economies, but very little fresh water. There are almost no permanent rivers, rainfall is low, and underground water reserves, which took thousands of years to accumulate, are being used far faster than nature can replace them. To survive and grow, the region has turned to the sea, which offers an almost unlimited supply of water, but only after the salt has been removed.","[2] Desalination, the process of removing salt from seawater, now supplies most of the drinking water in countries such as Saudi Arabia, the UAE, Kuwait and Qatar. Saudi Arabia is the world's largest producer of desalinated water, and plants such as Ras Al-Khair on the eastern coast can produce more than a million cubic metres of water a day, enough for millions of people. Huge pipelines then carry the water hundreds of kilometres inland to cities such as Riyadh.","[3] There are two main methods. Older plants mostly use thermal desalination: seawater is heated until it evaporates, and the steam is then cooled to produce fresh water. This method is reliable but consumes enormous amounts of energy. Newer plants increasingly use reverse osmosis, in which seawater is pushed at high pressure through extremely fine membranes that allow water to pass but block salt. Reverse osmosis typically uses less than half the energy of thermal methods, which explains why it now dominates new projects.","[4] Desalination is not without problems. Even with modern technology, it remains costly, and most plants still run on fossil fuels, adding to carbon emissions. In addition, the process produces brine, a very salty liquid that is usually returned to the sea. If brine is not diluted properly, it can harm marine life near the outlets.","[5] Engineers are now working on solutions. Several new plants in Saudi Arabia are powered partly by solar energy, and researchers are testing ways to extract valuable minerals from brine instead of discarding it. If these efforts succeed, desalination could become both cleaner and cheaper in the coming decades."],"q":[{"n":11,"s":"Which of the following best restates the sentence Underground water reserves are being used far faster than nature can replace them?","o":["Nature replaces underground water faster than people use it.","People are using underground water much more quickly than it can be naturally refilled.","Underground water is no longer used in the Gulf.","Underground water reserves are increasing every year."],"k":1,"e":"[paraphrase] used faster than replaced = used more quickly than refilled.","h":"الصياغة الصحيحة."},{"n":12,"s":"The word accumulate in paragraph 1 is closest in meaning to …","o":["gradually build up","disappear","become salty","flow quickly"],"k":0,"e":"[vocabulary in context] took thousands of years to accumulate.","h":"يتراكم."},{"n":13,"s":"Which country is the world's largest producer of desalinated water?","o":["Saudi Arabia","the UAE","Kuwait","Qatar"],"k":0,"e":"[detail] Saudi Arabia is the world's largest producer.","h":"السعودية."},{"n":14,"s":"Which of the following best restates the sentence This method is reliable but consumes enormous amounts of energy?","o":["Thermal desalination is unreliable and uses little energy.","Because thermal desalination uses energy, it is unreliable.","Thermal desalination is the cheapest and most reliable method.","Thermal desalination works dependably, although it uses a great deal of energy."],"k":3,"e":"[paraphrase] reliable + but + huge energy use.","h":"يحافظ على التضاد."},{"n":15,"s":"How does reverse osmosis remove salt?","o":["Seawater is pushed through very fine membranes that block salt.","Seawater is heated until it evaporates.","Salt is removed by adding chemicals.","Seawater is frozen and then melted."],"k":0,"e":"[detail] pushed at high pressure through extremely fine membranes.","h":"التناضح العكسي."},{"n":16,"s":"Which of the following best restates the sentence Reverse osmosis typically uses less than half the energy of thermal methods, which explains why it now dominates new projects?","o":["Most new plants choose reverse osmosis because it usually needs much less energy than thermal methods.","Reverse osmosis uses twice as much energy, so it is rarely used.","Thermal methods dominate new projects because they save energy.","Reverse osmosis and thermal methods use the same energy."],"k":0,"e":"[paraphrase] less than half energy → explains dominance.","h":"السبب والنتيجة."},{"n":17,"s":"The word dominates in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to …","o":["is banned from","is too expensive for","is the most common in","is slower than"],"k":2,"e":"[vocabulary in context] now dominates new projects.","h":"يهيمن على."},{"n":18,"s":"According to paragraph 4, brine can harm marine life when …","o":["it is turned into minerals","it is heated","it is stored on land","it is not diluted properly"],"k":3,"e":"[detail] If brine is not diluted properly, it can harm marine life.","h":"عند عدم التخفيف."},{"n":19,"s":"Which of the following best restates the sentence If these efforts succeed, desalination could become both cleaner and cheaper in the coming decades?","o":["Desalination will certainly become cleaner next year.","Successful research might make desalination less polluting and less expensive in future.","Desalination is already clean and cheap.","Even if the research succeeds, desalination will become more expensive."],"k":1,"e":"[paraphrase] condition (if) + possibility (could) + cleaner and cheaper.","h":"يحافظ على الشرط."},{"n":20,"s":"The author's attitude towards the future of desalination is best described as …","o":["completely negative","indifferent","certain of failure","cautiously hopeful"],"k":3,"e":"[attitude / tone] Engineers are working on solutions … If these efforts succeed …","h":"متفائل بحذر."}]},{"l":["Text 3 (Questions 21–30): The economics of food-delivery apps","(330 words)","[1] In many cities around the world, including Riyadh, Jeddah and Dammam, food-delivery apps have changed how people eat. With a few taps on a phone, customers can order meals from hundreds of restaurants and receive them within half an hour. The convenience is undeniable, and the market has grown rapidly, especially since 2020, when many people were unable to eat out. Today, delivery motorcycles have become a familiar sight on almost every major street.","[2] Yet behind the convenience lies a complicated business model. Delivery platforms usually charge restaurants a commission of between fifteen and thirty percent of each order. For a small restaurant, whose profit margin may be only ten percent, this can turn a profitable sale into a loss. Some restaurant owners say they feel trapped: if they leave the platform, they lose visibility to thousands of customers, but if they stay, they earn very little from delivery orders.","[3] Many restaurants respond by raising menu prices on the apps, so customers often pay more for the same meal than they would in the restaurant itself. Others have opened so-called dark kitchens, which have no dining area at all and exist only to prepare food for delivery. These reduce rent and staff costs, but they also mean that the friendly neighbourhood restaurant is slowly being replaced by an anonymous kitchen in an industrial area.","[4] Surprisingly, many of the largest platforms themselves have struggled to make a profit. They spend heavily on advertising, discounts and technology to win customers from competitors, and the cost of paying drivers is high. Some economists argue that the current model only survives because investors are willing to accept losses in the hope of controlling the market later.","[5] Delivery drivers, meanwhile, are usually treated as independent contractors rather than employees. This gives them flexibility to choose their working hours, but it also means that they often receive no health insurance or paid holidays. Several countries are now debating new laws to give these workers greater protection."],"q":[{"n":21,"s":"Which of the following best restates the sentence The convenience is undeniable, and the market has grown rapidly, especially since 2020?","o":["Some people deny that delivery apps are convenient.","The market grew rapidly before 2020 but has now stopped.","Delivery apps became less popular after 2020.","Delivery apps are clearly convenient, and their use has increased quickly, particularly in recent years."],"k":3,"e":"[paraphrase] undeniable = clearly; grown rapidly especially since 2020.","h":"الصياغة الصحيحة."},{"n":22,"s":"Which of the following best restates the sentence For a small restaurant, whose profit margin may be only ten percent, this can turn a profitable sale into a loss?","o":["Small restaurants make a thirty percent profit on every order.","The commission always makes small restaurants rich.","Because small restaurants earn little profit on each sale, the commission can make some delivery orders lose money.","Large restaurants lose money on every sale."],"k":2,"e":"[paraphrase] low margin + commission → loss.","h":"يحافظ على الشرط والنتيجة."},{"n":23,"s":"Why do some restaurant owners feel trapped?","o":["The apps refuse to let them leave by law.","Leaving the app means losing customers, but staying earns little.","They cannot cook fast enough.","Their rent is too high."],"k":1,"e":"[cause–effect] if they leave, they lose visibility … if they stay, they earn very little.","h":"الوقوع في فخ."},{"n":24,"s":"The word visibility in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to …","o":["the ability to see clearly","good weather","a delivery service","the chance to be seen"],"k":3,"e":"[vocabulary in context] lose visibility to thousands of customers.","h":"الظهور."},{"n":25,"s":"Which of the following best restates the sentence Customers often pay more for the same meal than they would in the restaurant itself?","o":["Meals in restaurants are always more expensive than on apps.","Apps sell different meals from restaurants.","Customers never pay more on apps.","The same food frequently costs more on the app than in the restaurant."],"k":3,"e":"[paraphrase] often pay more on apps.","h":"الصياغة الصحيحة."},{"n":26,"s":"What is a dark kitchen?","o":["a kitchen that only prepares food for delivery","a restaurant that opens only at night","a kitchen with no electricity","a restaurant in a shopping mall"],"k":0,"e":"[detail] no dining area … only to prepare food for delivery.","h":"المطبخ المظلم."},{"n":27,"s":"The author's attitude towards the spread of dark kitchens is best described as …","o":["enthusiastic","slightly regretful","angry","completely neutral"],"k":1,"e":"[attitude / tone] the friendly neighbourhood restaurant is slowly being replaced by an anonymous kitchen.","h":"أسف خفيف."},{"n":28,"s":"Which of the following best restates the sentence Some economists argue that the current model only survives because investors are willing to accept losses in the hope of controlling the market later?","o":["All economists agree that the platforms are very profitable.","According to some economists, the business continues only because investors accept losses now, hoping to dominate the market in future.","Investors refuse to accept losses, so the model is failing.","Economists believe that the platforms already control the market."],"k":1,"e":"[paraphrase] only survives because + hope of controlling later.","h":"يحافظ على الحصر والسبب."},{"n":29,"s":"According to paragraph 5, what is one disadvantage of being an independent contractor?","o":["Drivers often receive no health insurance or paid holidays.","Drivers cannot choose their hours.","Drivers must work in dark kitchens.","Drivers pay the restaurant's commission."],"k":0,"e":"[detail] receive no health insurance or paid holidays.","h":"سلبية."},{"n":30,"s":"Which is the best title for the passage?","o":["How to Open a Restaurant","The History of Fast Food","The Hidden Costs Behind Food-Delivery Apps","Why Drivers Prefer Motorcycles"],"k":2,"e":"[best title] Covers restaurants, prices, platforms and drivers.","h":"العنوان."}]}],"n":30},{"id":"4.4","t":"NOT / EXCEPT and Negative Questions","html":"<p><strong>Unit 4 · Lesson 4.4 · CEFR B2 · STEP link: Reading Comprehension (40%)</strong></p>\n<h3>Objectives</h3>\n<p>1. Solve negative questions by confirming three true options.</p>\n<p>2. Manage time on NOT / EXCEPT items.</p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><strong><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الأهداف</span></strong></p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">١. أن يحل الطالب الأسئلة السلبية بتأكيد صحة ثلاثة بدائل.</span></p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">٢. أن يدير وقته في أسئلة NOT / EXCEPT.</span></p>\n<h3>The Skill</h3>\n<p>Negative questions reverse the usual task. Which of the following is NOT mentioned …? / All of the following are true EXCEPT … / Which is NOT a reason …? Three options ARE in the passage; the answer is the one that is missing, false or not supported.</p>\n<p>Because the information for the three true options is often spread across several paragraphs, these questions can take longer. A good strategy is to treat them as a checklist: find and tick each true option, and the remaining option is the answer.</p>\n<p>Be careful with options that are almost right. An option may mention something from the passage but change one detail (a number, a place, a relationship) — that makes it false, so it could be the answer.</p>\n<p><strong>Typical STEP question stems:</strong></p>\n<p>▸ Which of the following is NOT mentioned as …?</p>\n<p>▸ All of the following are true EXCEPT …</p>\n<p>▸ Which of the following is NOT a reason for …?</p>\n<p>▸ According to the passage, … does NOT …</p>\n<p>▸ The passage mentions all of the following EXCEPT …</p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><strong><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المهارة</span></strong></p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الأسئلة السلبية تعكس المهمة المعتادة: ثلاثة بدائل موجودة أو صحيحة في النص، والإجابة هي البديل الغائب أو الخاطئ أو غير المدعوم. وبما أن المعلومات قد تتوزع على عدة فقرات فعاملها كقائمة تحقق: ضع علامة على كل بديل صحيح، والبديل الباقي هو الإجابة. واحذر البديل الذي يذكر شيئًا من النص لكنه يغيّر تفصيلة واحدة.</span></p>\n<h3>Strategy</h3>\n<blockquote>\n<p>1. Circle NOT or EXCEPT so you do not forget the question is negative.</p>\n<p>2. Find the paragraph(s) about the topic of the question.</p>\n<p>3. Tick each option you can prove is in the passage and correct.</p>\n<p>4. Check every detail of the remaining option: is it missing, or does it change a fact?</p>\n<p>5. If time is short, leave the NOT question and return to it — it is often the slowest in a set.</p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">ضع دائرة حول NOT أو EXCEPT، وحدد الفقرات المتعلقة بالسؤال، وضع علامة على كل بديل تثبت صحته، ثم افحص البديل الباقي: هل هو غائب أم يغيّر حقيقة؟ وإن ضاق الوقت فأجّل هذا السؤال وعد إليه.</span></p>\n</blockquote>\n<h3>Traps and Distractor Types</h3>\n<blockquote>\n<p>✗ Forgetting the question is negative and choosing the first true option.</p>\n<p>✗ Choosing an option that is in the passage but in a different paragraph.</p>\n<p>✗ Missing a small change in a number or a place.</p>\n<p>✗ Choosing an option that is true in real life but not mentioned — it IS the answer if the question asks NOT mentioned.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h3>Worked Example (not counted in the quiz)</h3>\n<p><strong>Example text: The Date Palm</strong></p>\n<p><em>(50 words)</em></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>[1]</strong> The date palm has been called the tree of life in Arabia. Its fruit provides energy and important minerals, its leaves are woven into mats and baskets, and its trunk was traditionally used to build roofs. Even the seeds were roasted and ground to make a drink similar to coffee.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Example 1. All of the following parts of the date palm are mentioned as useful EXCEPT …</strong></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>A. the fruit B. the leaves C. the roots D. the seeds</p>\n<p><strong>Think:</strong> Tick: fruit ✓ (energy), leaves ✓ (mats), seeds ✓ (drink).</p>\n<p><strong>Think:</strong> Roots are not mentioned.</p>\n<p><strong>Think:</strong> The answer is C.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">ضع علامة على كل ما ذُكر، والبديل الباقي (الجذور) هو الإجابة.</span></p>","wu":"Worked example: Example 1 C","quiz":[{"l":["Read each text and answer the questions. Choose the best answer (A, B, C or D).","اقرأ كل نص ثم أجب عن الأسئلة التي تليه، واختر الإجابة الصحيحة من بين البدائل الأربعة.","Text 1 (Questions 1–10): The camel's physical adaptations to desert life","(346 words)","[1] Few animals are as well suited to their environment as the dromedary, or Arabian camel. For thousands of years, it has carried people and goods across deserts where temperatures can rise above 50°C during the day and fall close to freezing at night. Its survival depends on a remarkable set of physical adaptations, many of which scientists are still studying. Some of these adaptations are visible from the outside, while others work quietly inside the camel's body, in its blood, kidneys and brain.","[2] The most famous feature, the hump, is often misunderstood. It does not store water, as many people believe, but fat, which can weigh up to thirty-five kilograms. When food is scarce, the camel breaks down this fat for energy. Storing fat in one place, rather than under the skin all over the body, also helps the animal lose heat more easily. A camel that has gone without food for a long time has a small, soft hump that may even lean to one side.","[3] Camels are also extraordinarily efficient with water. They can lose up to a quarter of their body weight through dehydration without serious harm, a loss that would kill most mammals. When water is available, a thirsty camel can drink more than a hundred litres in about ten minutes. Their kidneys produce very concentrated urine, and their noses capture moisture from the air they breathe out.","[4] Other adaptations protect the camel from sand and sun. Two rows of long eyelashes and a thin third eyelid keep sand out of its eyes, while its nostrils can close completely during sandstorms. Thick pads on its chest and knees allow it to kneel on hot sand without being burned, and its wide, soft feet spread out to stop it from sinking.","[5] Perhaps most surprisingly, a camel's body temperature is not fixed. It can rise by about six degrees during the day before the animal starts to sweat. This allows the camel to save water that it would otherwise lose by sweating, and it releases the stored heat during the cool desert night."],"q":[{"n":1,"s":"All of the following are true about the camel's hump EXCEPT …","o":["it stores fat","it stores water for long journeys","it can weigh up to thirty-five kilograms","it helps the camel lose heat"],"k":1,"e":"[NOT / EXCEPT] It does not store water … but fat.","h":"لا يخزن الماء."},{"n":2,"s":"Which of the following is NOT mentioned as a way camels save or obtain water?","o":["drinking seawater","producing concentrated urine","capturing moisture when breathing out","allowing body temperature to rise"],"k":0,"e":"[NOT / EXCEPT] urine ✓ noses ✓ temperature ✓; seawater ✗.","h":"غير مذكور."},{"n":3,"s":"The word scarce in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to …","o":["poisonous","very fresh","expensive","difficult to find"],"k":3,"e":"[vocabulary in context] When food is scarce, the camel breaks down fat.","h":"شحيح."},{"n":4,"s":"How much water can a thirsty camel drink in about ten minutes?","o":["about ten litres","thirty-five litres","more than a hundred litres","a quarter of a litre"],"k":2,"e":"[detail] drink more than a hundred litres in about ten minutes.","h":"أكثر من 100 لتر."},{"n":5,"s":"All of the following protect the camel from sand EXCEPT …","o":["long eyelashes","a third eyelid","a thick layer of fat under the skin","nostrils that can close"],"k":2,"e":"[NOT / EXCEPT] eyelashes ✓ eyelid ✓ nostrils ✓; fat under skin ✗ (fat is in hump).","h":"لا يحمي من الرمل."},{"n":6,"s":"What is the purpose of the thick pads on the camel's chest and knees?","o":["to store water","to help it run faster","to keep sand out of its eyes","to let it kneel on hot sand without burning"],"k":3,"e":"[detail] allow it to kneel on hot sand without being burned.","h":"الوسائد السميكة."},{"n":7,"s":"Which of the following is NOT stated about camels' feet?","o":["They are wide.","They have sharp claws for digging.","They are soft.","They stop the camel from sinking."],"k":1,"e":"[NOT / EXCEPT] wide ✓ soft ✓ stop sinking ✓; claws ✗.","h":"غير مذكور."},{"n":8,"s":"Why does the camel's body temperature rise during the day?","o":["so that it does not need to sweat and lose water","because it is ill","because it eats more at noon","to help it see in the dark"],"k":0,"e":"[cause–effect] This allows the camel to save water that it would otherwise lose by sweating.","h":"توفير الماء."},{"n":9,"s":"The phrase Perhaps most surprisingly in paragraph 5 suggests that the author …","o":["doubts the research","thinks camels are dangerous","has seen this in many animals","finds this adaptation especially unexpected"],"k":3,"e":"[attitude / tone] Perhaps most surprisingly = the most unexpected feature.","h":"الأكثر إثارة للدهشة."},{"n":10,"s":"Which is the best title for the passage?","o":["How the Camel Survives in the Desert","The Fastest Animals in Arabia","Why Deserts Are Cold at Night","Camel Racing in Saudi Arabia"],"k":0,"e":"[best title] The passage describes the camel's adaptations.","h":"العنوان."}]},{"l":["Text 2 (Questions 11–20): The ancient incense route of Arabia","(334 words)","[1] More than two thousand years ago, long caravans of camels crossed the Arabian Peninsula carrying some of the most valuable goods in the ancient world: frankincense and myrrh. These aromatic resins, produced from trees that grow mainly in southern Arabia and the Horn of Africa, were burned in temples, used in medicines and perfumes, and offered as gifts to kings. In some periods, they were worth as much as gold, and the demand from Egypt, Mesopotamia and later Rome seemed almost endless. Whoever controlled their transport could become extremely wealthy.","[2] The incense route ran for around 2,000 kilometres, from the frankincense lands of Dhofar and Hadramawt in the south, through Najran and the Hejaz, to ports on the Mediterranean such as Gaza. A one-way journey could take two to three months. Caravans travelled in stages between oasis towns, where they could rest, find water and trade. Guides who knew the desert and the locations of wells were essential, because a wrong turn could mean death.","[3] These towns grew rich by providing services to the traders and by charging taxes on the goods that passed through. The Nabataeans, whose great city of Petra lies in modern Jordan, controlled the northern part of the route and built a second major city at Hegra, near today's AlUla. Their skill in collecting and storing rainwater allowed them to settle in places that seemed too dry for permanent life.","[4] The trade also spread ideas. Merchants carried languages, religions, building styles and writing systems along with their goods. Inscriptions found along the route are written in several ancient scripts, showing how many different peoples met and exchanged knowledge there.","[5] The incense route declined from the first century onward. Sailors had learned to use the monsoon winds to travel directly by sea from southern Arabia and India to Egypt, which was faster and cheaper than the overland journey. In 2005, UNESCO listed several sites linked to the route as World Heritage, and today Hegra attracts visitors from around the world."],"q":[{"n":11,"s":"The passage mentions all of the following uses of frankincense and myrrh EXCEPT …","o":["in temples","as food for camels","in medicines","as gifts to kings"],"k":1,"e":"[NOT / EXCEPT] temples ✓ medicines ✓ gifts ✓; camel food ✗.","h":"غير مذكور."},{"n":12,"s":"The word aromatic in paragraph 1 is closest in meaning to …","o":["very heavy","made of metal","very old","having a strong, pleasant smell"],"k":3,"e":"[vocabulary in context] aromatic resins … burned in temples, used in perfumes.","h":"عطري."},{"n":13,"s":"Which of the following places is NOT mentioned as part of the route?","o":["Najran","Dhofar","Makkah","Gaza"],"k":2,"e":"[NOT / EXCEPT] Dhofar ✓ Najran ✓ Gaza ✓; Makkah not mentioned.","h":"غير مذكور."},{"n":14,"s":"How long could a one-way journey along the route take?","o":["two to three months","two to three weeks","about a year","ten days"],"k":0,"e":"[detail] A one-way journey could take two to three months.","h":"شهران إلى ثلاثة."},{"n":15,"s":"All of the following are given as ways the oasis towns benefited from the trade EXCEPT …","o":["providing services to the traders","selling frankincense trees to the traders","charging taxes on goods","trading with the caravans"],"k":1,"e":"[NOT / EXCEPT] services ✓ taxes ✓ trade ✓; selling trees ✗.","h":"ليس من الفوائد."},{"n":16,"s":"What allowed the Nabataeans to live in very dry places?","o":["their large army","rivers near Petra","trade with India","their skill in collecting and storing rainwater"],"k":3,"e":"[cause–effect] Their skill in collecting and storing rainwater allowed them to settle.","h":"حصاد المياه."},{"n":17,"s":"Which of the following is NOT mentioned as something spread along the route?","o":["languages","religions","musical instruments","writing systems"],"k":2,"e":"[NOT / EXCEPT] languages ✓ religions ✓ building styles ✓ writing ✓; music ✗.","h":"غير مذكور."},{"n":18,"s":"Why does the author mention inscriptions in several ancient scripts?","o":["to explain how incense was made","to describe the taxes paid","to show that traders could not read","to show that many peoples met and exchanged ideas"],"k":3,"e":"[purpose] showing how many different peoples met and exchanged knowledge.","h":"دليل على التبادل."},{"n":19,"s":"Why did the incense route decline?","o":["The frankincense trees disappeared.","Sea travel using monsoon winds became faster and cheaper.","UNESCO closed the route.","Camels became too expensive."],"k":1,"e":"[cause–effect] use the monsoon winds … faster and cheaper than the overland journey.","h":"سبب التراجع."},{"n":20,"s":"All of the following are true according to the passage EXCEPT …","o":["Petra is in modern Jordan.","UNESCO listed sites linked to the route.","Hegra was built by the Romans.","Hegra is near AlUla."],"k":2,"e":"[NOT / EXCEPT] Hegra was built by the Nabataeans, not the Romans.","h":"خطأ في النص."}]},{"l":["Text 3 (Questions 21–30): The volcanic fields of Harrat Khaybar","(328 words)","[1] In the north-west of Saudi Arabia, between Madinah and Tabuk, lies one of the largest volcanic fields in the Middle East: Harrat Khaybar. Covering about 12,000 square kilometres, it is a dramatic landscape of black lava flows, cones and craters. Seen from space, the dark basalt stands out sharply against the pale sand around it. Many of the lava flows are so rough and broken that it is almost impossible to walk across them, and in summer the black rock becomes extremely hot.","[2] The field was formed over millions of years as hot magma rose through cracks in the Earth's crust, which is slowly being pulled apart in this region by the same forces that created the Red Sea. Harrat Khaybar contains more than 400 volcanic cones. Among the most impressive is Jabal Al-Qidr, a steep black cone that rises above the surrounding lava, and Jabal Al-Abyad, a rare white volcano made of a lighter-coloured rock.","[3] Although the area seems silent today, it is not completely extinct. Geologists believe the most recent eruptions took place within the last 1,500 years, and historical Arabic sources describe volcanic activity in the wider region, including a major eruption near Madinah in 1256. For this reason, the Saudi Geological Survey continues to monitor earthquakes and ground movement in the volcanic fields of the west.","[4] Harrat Khaybar is also of great interest to archaeologists. Satellite images have revealed thousands of mysterious stone structures across the lava fields, including long walls known as kites, which were used to trap wild animals, and circular structures that may have been tombs. Some of these are thought to be more than 7,000 years old, older than the pyramids of Egypt.","[5] Despite the harsh conditions, the area supports wildlife such as foxes, wolves and eagles. Visitors who make the difficult journey are rewarded with some of the most striking scenery in Arabia, but they are advised to travel with a guide and a suitable vehicle."],"q":[{"n":21,"s":"All of the following are mentioned as features of Harrat Khaybar EXCEPT …","o":["lava flows","hot springs","cones","craters"],"k":1,"e":"[NOT / EXCEPT] lava ✓ cones ✓ craters ✓; hot springs ✗.","h":"غير مذكور."},{"n":22,"s":"How large is Harrat Khaybar?","o":["about 12,000 square kilometres","about 400 square kilometres","about 1,500 square kilometres","about 7,000 square kilometres"],"k":0,"e":"[detail] Covering about 12,000 square kilometres.","h":"المساحة."},{"n":23,"s":"According to paragraph 2, what formed the field?","o":["a meteorite hitting the Earth","magma rising through cracks in a crust that is being pulled apart","rivers carrying black sand","an earthquake in 1256"],"k":1,"e":"[cause–effect] magma rose through cracks … crust … being pulled apart.","h":"التكوين."},{"n":24,"s":"What makes Jabal Al-Abyad unusual?","o":["It is the tallest mountain in Arabia.","It is still erupting.","It is a white volcano made of lighter rock.","It is covered with trees."],"k":2,"e":"[detail] a rare white volcano made of a lighter-coloured rock.","h":"البركان الأبيض."},{"n":25,"s":"The word extinct in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to …","o":["very dangerous","no longer active","recently discovered","completely hidden"],"k":1,"e":"[vocabulary in context] not completely extinct … recent eruptions.","h":"خامد تمامًا."},{"n":26,"s":"Which of the following is NOT given as evidence that the area may still be active?","o":["lava flowing in the area today","eruptions within the last 1,500 years","historical Arabic descriptions of activity","an eruption near Madinah in 1256"],"k":0,"e":"[NOT / EXCEPT] No lava flows today are mentioned; the area seems silent.","h":"ليس دليلًا مذكورًا."},{"n":27,"s":"Why does the Saudi Geological Survey monitor the area?","o":["to find gold","to build new cities","because future volcanic activity is possible","to protect wolves"],"k":2,"e":"[cause–effect] For this reason (not extinct), the Survey continues to monitor.","h":"احتمال النشاط."},{"n":28,"s":"All of the following are true about the stone structures EXCEPT …","o":["some are over 7,000 years old","some may have been tombs","they were all built in the last century","kites were used to trap animals"],"k":2,"e":"[NOT / EXCEPT] Some are more than 7,000 years old — not recent.","h":"خطأ."},{"n":29,"s":"Which animal is NOT mentioned as living in the area?","o":["foxes","wolves","eagles","camels"],"k":3,"e":"[NOT / EXCEPT] foxes ✓ wolves ✓ eagles ✓; camels ✗.","h":"غير مذكور."},{"n":30,"s":"It can be inferred from the last paragraph that reaching Harrat Khaybar …","o":["is not easy","is possible only by plane","is forbidden for tourists","takes only a few minutes from Madinah"],"k":0,"e":"[inference] Visitors who make the difficult journey … travel with a guide and a suitable vehicle.","h":"صعب الوصول."}]}],"n":30},{"id":"4.5","t":"Text Organization: Compare–Contrast, Problem–Solution, Cause–Effect","html":"<p><strong>Unit 4 · Lesson 4.5 · CEFR B2 · STEP link: Reading Comprehension (40%)</strong></p>\n<h3>Objectives</h3>\n<p>1. Recognize compare–contrast, problem–solution and cause–effect organization.</p>\n<p>2. Answer questions about how a passage or paragraph is organized.</p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><strong><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الأهداف</span></strong></p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">١. أن يتعرف الطالب على أنماط التنظيم: المقارنة، والمشكلة والحل، والسبب والنتيجة.</span></p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">٢. أن يجيب عن أسئلة طريقة تنظيم النص أو الفقرة.</span></p>\n<h3>The Skill</h3>\n<p>Academic texts follow common patterns. Compare–contrast: two things are shown to be similar or different (similarly, likewise, whereas, on the other hand, unlike). Problem–solution: a problem is described, then one or more solutions (one way to …, to address this, a remedy). Cause–effect: reasons and results (leads to, results in, as a consequence, due to).</p>\n<p>STEP asks: How is the passage organized? / What is the function of paragraph 3? / The author develops paragraph 2 mainly by … Typical answers: by giving examples, by comparing two methods, by describing a process, by presenting a problem and possible solutions.</p>\n<p>Knowing the pattern helps you predict what comes next and locate answers faster.</p>\n<p><strong>Typical STEP question stems:</strong></p>\n<p>▸ How is the passage mainly organized?</p>\n<p>▸ Which of the following best describes the organization of paragraph 2?</p>\n<p>▸ The author develops paragraph 3 mainly by …</p>\n<p>▸ What is the relationship between paragraphs 2 and 3?</p>\n<p>▸ In paragraph 4, the author …</p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><strong><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المهارة</span></strong></p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">تتبع النصوص الأكاديمية أنماطًا شائعة: المقارنة (similarly، whereas، unlike)، والمشكلة والحل (to address this، one way to)، والسبب والنتيجة (leads to، as a consequence، due to). ويسأل الاختبار: كيف نُظّم النص؟ وما وظيفة الفقرة الثالثة؟ ومعرفة النمط تساعدك على توقّع ما يأتي وإيجاد الإجابات بسرعة.</span></p>\n<h3>Strategy</h3>\n<blockquote>\n<p>1. Read the first sentence of each paragraph and label it: problem, cause, effect, solution, similarity, difference, example.</p>\n<p>2. Look for signal words that reveal the pattern.</p>\n<p>3. For a whole-passage question, look at the sequence of your labels (problem → causes → solutions).</p>\n<p>4. For a paragraph question, ask: What job does this paragraph do for the whole text?</p>\n<p>5. Reject options that describe a pattern not present (e.g., chronological order when there are no dates).</p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">اقرأ الجملة الأولى من كل فقرة وصنّفها (مشكلة، سبب، نتيجة، حل، تشابه، اختلاف، مثال)، وابحث عن كلمات الربط، ثم انظر إلى تسلسل التصنيفات لتحديد تنظيم النص كله، واسأل عن وظيفة كل فقرة.</span></p>\n</blockquote>\n<h3>Traps and Distractor Types</h3>\n<blockquote>\n<p>✗ Choosing chronological order because there is one date.</p>\n<p>✗ Confusing cause–effect with problem–solution.</p>\n<p>✗ Describing one paragraph's pattern instead of the whole passage.</p>\n<p>✗ Choosing compare–contrast when only one thing is described.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h3>Worked Example (not counted in the quiz)</h3>\n<p><strong>Example text: Two Ways to Learn Vocabulary</strong></p>\n<p><em>(48 words)</em></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>[1]</strong> Some learners memorize word lists, while others learn words by reading stories. Word lists are fast and organized, whereas reading is slower but shows how words are really used. Unlike lists, stories also help learners remember words through context and emotion. Many teachers therefore recommend combining both methods.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Example 1. How is the paragraph mainly organized?</strong></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>A. as a series of events in time order</p>\n<p>B. by comparing and contrasting two methods</p>\n<p>C. by describing a problem and its causes</p>\n<p>D. by giving the history of word lists</p>\n<p><strong>Think:</strong> Signal words: while, whereas, Unlike.</p>\n<p><strong>Think:</strong> Two methods are compared.</p>\n<p><strong>Think:</strong> The answer is B.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">كلمات while وwhereas وUnlike تدل على نمط المقارنة.</span></p>","wu":"Worked example: Example 1 B","quiz":[{"l":["Read each text and answer the questions. Choose the best answer (A, B, C or D).","اقرأ كل نص ثم أجب عن الأسئلة التي تليه، واختر الإجابة الصحيحة من بين البدائل الأربعة.","Text 1 (Questions 1–10): Urban heat islands: problems and solutions","(332 words)","[1] On a summer afternoon, the centre of a large city can be several degrees hotter than the countryside just a few kilometres away. In some cases, the difference reaches eight or even ten degrees at night. Scientists call this phenomenon the urban heat island, and it is becoming a serious problem in fast-growing cities such as Riyadh, Cairo and Phoenix in the United States. As cities expand and summers become hotter, the effect is likely to grow stronger unless planners act.","[2] Several factors cause it. Dark surfaces such as asphalt roads and roofs absorb large amounts of sunlight and release the heat slowly after sunset. Tall buildings trap warm air and block the wind that would otherwise cool the streets. Air conditioners, cars and factories add even more heat. At the same time, cities usually have far fewer trees and green spaces than rural areas, so there is less shade and less cooling from plants.","[3] The consequences are significant. Higher temperatures increase the demand for air conditioning, which raises electricity bills and, if power comes from fossil fuels, increases pollution. Heat waves become more dangerous, especially for elderly people, outdoor workers and people with heart or breathing problems. Hot air can also worsen air quality by speeding up the formation of ground-level ozone.","[4] Fortunately, cities can take action. One of the simplest solutions is to plant more trees, which provide shade and cool the air through evaporation. The Green Riyadh programme, for example, aims to plant millions of trees across the capital. Another approach is to use cool roofs and pavements made from light-coloured or reflective materials, which can lower surface temperatures by twenty degrees or more. Designing streets to allow wind to flow, and adding fountains and shaded walkways, can also help.","[5] None of these measures will remove urban heat islands completely, but together they can make cities noticeably cooler and healthier. As more of the world's population moves to cities, urban planners can no longer afford to ignore this problem."],"q":[{"n":1,"s":"How is the passage mainly organized?","o":["A problem is described, followed by its causes, effects and solutions.","Events are described in time order.","Two cities are compared in detail.","A single experiment is described step by step."],"k":0,"e":"[organization] Para 1 problem; 2 causes; 3 consequences; 4 solutions.","h":"مشكلة–أسباب–آثار–حلول."},{"n":2,"s":"What is the function of paragraph 2?","o":["to suggest solutions","to compare Riyadh and Cairo","to explain the causes of urban heat islands","to describe health effects"],"k":2,"e":"[insertion / paragraph function] Several factors cause it.","h":"وظيفة الفقرة 2."},{"n":3,"s":"Which of the following is NOT given as a cause of urban heat islands?","o":["dark surfaces","tall buildings","high levels of rainfall","fewer trees"],"k":2,"e":"[NOT / EXCEPT] Rain is not mentioned as a cause.","h":"ليس سببًا."},{"n":4,"s":"The word phenomenon in paragraph 1 is closest in meaning to …","o":["a type of building","something that happens and can be observed","a dangerous disease","a weather forecast"],"k":1,"e":"[vocabulary in context] Scientists call this phenomenon the urban heat island.","h":"ظاهرة."},{"n":5,"s":"Paragraph 3 is mainly organized around …","o":["the history of air conditioning","solutions to air pollution","the effects of higher urban temperatures","a comparison of fuels"],"k":2,"e":"[organization] The consequences are significant …","h":"الآثار."},{"n":6,"s":"Which group is NOT mentioned as especially at risk during heat waves?","o":["young children","elderly people","outdoor workers","people with heart problems"],"k":0,"e":"[NOT / EXCEPT] elderly ✓ outdoor workers ✓ heart ✓; children ✗.","h":"غير مذكور."},{"n":7,"s":"The word Fortunately at the start of paragraph 4 signals a move from …","o":["causes to effects","one city to another","facts to opinions","problems to solutions"],"k":3,"e":"[organization] Fortunately, cities can take action.","h":"انتقال للحلول."},{"n":8,"s":"Why does the author mention the Green Riyadh programme?","o":["as an example of the tree-planting solution","to criticize Riyadh's planners","to compare Riyadh with Phoenix","to explain why cities are hot"],"k":0,"e":"[purpose] The Green Riyadh programme, for example …","h":"مثال على الحل."},{"n":9,"s":"According to paragraph 4, reflective materials can …","o":["lower surface temperatures by twenty degrees or more","increase wind speed","produce electricity","remove all urban heat"],"k":0,"e":"[detail] can lower surface temperatures by twenty degrees or more.","h":"تخفيض الحرارة."},{"n":10,"s":"The author's attitude in the final paragraph is best described as …","o":["hopeless","realistic but urgent","humorous","indifferent"],"k":1,"e":"[attitude / tone] None will remove completely … can no longer afford to ignore.","h":"واقعي وعاجل."}]},{"l":["Text 2 (Questions 11–20): Solar and wind energy compared","(328 words)","[1] As countries try to reduce their dependence on oil and gas, two sources of renewable energy have grown faster than any others: solar power and wind power. Both are clean, both use free natural resources, and both have become much cheaper over the past fifteen years. Yet they differ in important ways, and understanding these differences helps explain why most countries use a mixture of the two.","[2] Solar power converts sunlight into electricity, usually through photovoltaic panels. Its greatest advantage in Saudi Arabia is obvious: the country receives some of the strongest and most reliable sunshine on Earth. Solar panels have no moving parts, so they need little maintenance, and they can be installed almost anywhere, from huge desert farms to the roofs of homes. However, they produce nothing at night and less on cloudy or dusty days, and sand on the panels must be cleaned regularly.","[3] Wind power, by contrast, uses the movement of air to turn large turbines. Unlike solar panels, turbines can generate electricity day and night, as long as the wind blows. Coastal and mountainous areas, such as parts of the north-west of the Kingdom, have strong and steady winds. On the other hand, turbines are large, need more maintenance and are sometimes criticized for their noise and their effect on birds. Their output also varies, since wind speed changes from hour to hour.","[4] Because the strengths of one often match the weaknesses of the other, the two technologies work well together. In many desert regions, the wind tends to be stronger in the evening and at night, exactly when solar output falls. Saudi Arabia's first large wind farm, at Dumat Al-Jandal, began operating in 2022 alongside a growing number of solar plants.","[5] The main challenge for both remains storage. Since neither the sun nor the wind can be switched on when demand is high, large batteries and other storage systems will be essential if renewables are to supply most of a country's electricity."],"q":[{"n":11,"s":"How is the passage mainly organized?","o":["by describing a problem and one solution","by telling the history of oil","by listing steps in a process","by comparing and contrasting two energy sources"],"k":3,"e":"[organization] Similarities in para 1; solar; wind by contrast; working together.","h":"مقارنة."},{"n":12,"s":"Which of the following is mentioned as a similarity between solar and wind power?","o":["Both work at night.","Both have no moving parts.","Both need sand cleaning.","Both have become much cheaper."],"k":3,"e":"[detail] both have become much cheaper over the past fifteen years.","h":"تشابه."},{"n":13,"s":"The phrase by contrast in paragraph 3 signals that the author is going to …","o":["give another advantage of solar power","describe differences from solar power","explain the history of turbines","conclude the passage"],"k":1,"e":"[organization] Wind power, by contrast …","h":"إشارة إلى الاختلاف."},{"n":14,"s":"Which of the following is NOT mentioned as a disadvantage of solar panels?","o":["They make a lot of noise.","They produce nothing at night.","They produce less on dusty days.","Sand must be cleaned off them."],"k":0,"e":"[NOT / EXCEPT] Noise is mentioned for turbines, not panels.","h":"ليس عيبًا للطاقة الشمسية."},{"n":15,"s":"All of the following are given as disadvantages of wind turbines EXCEPT …","o":["they need more maintenance","they may affect birds","they cannot work at night","their output varies"],"k":2,"e":"[NOT / EXCEPT] Turbines CAN generate day and night.","h":"ليس عيبًا للرياح."},{"n":16,"s":"The word maintenance in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to …","o":["work done to keep something in good condition","the cost of building","a type of energy","a government rule"],"k":0,"e":"[vocabulary in context] no moving parts, so they need little maintenance.","h":"صيانة."},{"n":17,"s":"What is the function of paragraph 4?","o":["to describe the disadvantages of wind","to explain why the two sources complement each other","to introduce the topic","to describe storage technology"],"k":1,"e":"[insertion / paragraph function] Because the strengths of one often match the weaknesses of the other …","h":"التكامل."},{"n":18,"s":"According to the passage, why does wind complement solar power in desert regions?","o":["Wind farms are cheaper than solar farms.","Wind is often stronger when solar output falls.","Wind turbines clean the solar panels.","Solar panels power the wind turbines."],"k":1,"e":"[cause–effect] wind tends to be stronger in the evening and at night.","h":"التوقيت."},{"n":19,"s":"Where is Saudi Arabia's first large wind farm?","o":["Dumat Al-Jandal","Riyadh","the Red Sea coast","Al-Ahsa"],"k":0,"e":"[detail] at Dumat Al-Jandal, began operating in 2022.","h":"دومة الجندل."},{"n":20,"s":"It can be inferred from the last paragraph that, without good storage, …","o":["solar panels will stop working","wind turbines will become cheaper","oil will no longer be needed","renewables will struggle to meet demand at all times"],"k":3,"e":"[inference] storage will be essential if renewables are to supply most electricity.","h":"التخزين ضروري."}]},{"l":["Text 3 (Questions 21–30): Causes and effects of soil erosion","(352 words)","[1] Soil may look like ordinary dirt, but it is one of the most valuable resources on the planet. It takes between several hundred and a thousand years to form just a few centimetres of fertile topsoil, yet that same layer can be lost in a few years through erosion — the removal of soil by wind and water. Without healthy soil, it is impossible to grow most of the food that the world depends on, which is why scientists are increasingly worried about how quickly it is disappearing.","[2] Erosion is a natural process, but human activity has accelerated it dramatically. The most important cause is the removal of vegetation. When forests are cut down or grasslands are overgrazed by too many sheep, goats and camels, the roots that once held the soil together disappear. Poor farming practices, such as ploughing up and down slopes rather than across them, allow rainwater to wash soil away. In dry regions, strong winds can lift loose topsoil and carry it hundreds of kilometres.","[3] The effects are wide-ranging. Farmers lose the most fertile part of their land, and crop yields fall. Soil washed into rivers and reservoirs makes the water muddy and reduces the storage capacity of dams. In arid regions, erosion contributes to desertification, turning once-productive land into desert. Dust storms, which are frequent in the Arabian Peninsula, are partly made worse by eroded soil, and they can cause breathing problems and disrupt transport.","[4] As a result of these effects, some experts describe soil erosion as a quiet crisis: it happens slowly, rarely makes headlines, but threatens the food supply of millions of people. The United Nations estimates that a third of the world's soils are already moderately or highly degraded.","[5] Several methods can reduce erosion, including planting trees as windbreaks, controlling grazing, and farming along the contours of hills. In Saudi Arabia, the national programme to plant ten billion trees is partly intended to protect soil and reduce dust storms. Farmers are also being encouraged to use drip irrigation and to leave crop remains on fields after harvest, so that the ground is never left bare."],"q":[{"n":21,"s":"How is the passage mainly organized?","o":["It compares two farming methods.","It describes historical events in order.","It explains the causes and effects of a process and mentions solutions.","It presents one person's opinion about farming."],"k":2,"e":"[organization] Para 2 causes; 3 effects; 4 summary; 5 methods.","h":"سبب ونتيجة."},{"n":22,"s":"Why does the author mention the time it takes to form topsoil?","o":["to explain how farmers plough","to describe a dust storm","to compare soil and water","to show how valuable and hard to replace it is"],"k":3,"e":"[purpose] hundreds to a thousand years to form … lost in a few years.","h":"إبراز القيمة."},{"n":23,"s":"The word accelerated in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to …","o":["stopped","made faster","studied","hidden"],"k":1,"e":"[vocabulary in context] human activity has accelerated it dramatically.","h":"سرّع."},{"n":24,"s":"According to paragraph 2, what is the most important cause of increased erosion?","o":["heavy rainfall","the building of dams","the removal of vegetation","dust storms"],"k":2,"e":"[cause–effect] The most important cause is the removal of vegetation.","h":"إزالة الغطاء النباتي."},{"n":25,"s":"Paragraph 3 is mainly organized as …","o":["a list of effects of erosion","a list of causes of erosion","a comparison of two regions","a step-by-step process"],"k":0,"e":"[organization] The effects are wide-ranging … farmers … rivers … desertification … dust storms.","h":"قائمة آثار."},{"n":26,"s":"Which of the following is NOT mentioned as an effect of erosion?","o":["lower crop yields","muddy rivers","worse dust storms","rising sea levels"],"k":3,"e":"[NOT / EXCEPT] Sea levels not mentioned.","h":"ليس أثرًا مذكورًا."},{"n":27,"s":"The phrase As a result of these effects at the beginning of paragraph 4 shows that paragraph 4 …","o":["introduces a new cause","draws a conclusion from paragraph 3","contradicts paragraph 3","gives an unrelated example"],"k":1,"e":"[organization] As a result of these effects, experts describe it as a quiet crisis.","h":"استنتاج."},{"n":28,"s":"Why do some experts call soil erosion a quiet crisis?","o":["It does not make any noise.","It only affects quiet villages.","It has already been solved.","It happens slowly and gets little attention but is very serious."],"k":3,"e":"[inference] happens slowly, rarely makes headlines, but threatens the food supply.","h":"أزمة صامتة."},{"n":29,"s":"The word degraded in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to …","o":["improved","damaged and lower in quality","measured","covered with trees"],"k":1,"e":"[vocabulary in context] moderately or highly degraded soils.","h":"متدهور."},{"n":30,"s":"Which method to reduce erosion is NOT mentioned in paragraph 5?","o":["planting windbreaks","controlling grazing","building more dams","farming along contours"],"k":2,"e":"[NOT / EXCEPT] windbreaks ✓ grazing ✓ contours ✓; dams ✗.","h":"غير مذكور."}]}],"n":30},{"id":"4.6","t":"Reading Academic Passages: A Mixed STEP Set","html":"<p><strong>Unit 4 · Lesson 4.6 · CEFR B2 · STEP link: Reading Comprehension (40%)</strong></p>\n<h3>Objectives</h3>\n<p>1. Read academic passages on science and social science efficiently.</p>\n<p>2. Handle a mixed set of STEP question types on one passage.</p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><strong><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الأهداف</span></strong></p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">١. أن يقرأ الطالب النصوص الأكاديمية العلمية والاجتماعية بكفاءة.</span></p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">٢. أن يتعامل مع مجموعة متنوعة من أنواع أسئلة STEP على نص واحد.</span></p>\n<h3>The Skill</h3>\n<p>In the real STEP Reading section, every passage is followed by a MIXED set of questions: main idea, detail, vocabulary, reference, inference, purpose, NOT/EXCEPT and paraphrase. You must recognize each type quickly and switch strategy.</p>\n<p>Academic passages contain technical words, numbers and long sentences. Do not stop at every difficult word. First skim the passage (about one minute) to understand its topic and organization; then answer the questions, returning to the exact place in the text each time.</p>\n<p>Questions usually follow the order of the passage, except whole-passage questions (main idea, title, purpose, organization), which are often at the beginning or end of the set.</p>\n<p><strong>Typical STEP question stems:</strong></p>\n<p>▸ What is the main idea of the passage?</p>\n<p>▸ The word … in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to …</p>\n<p>▸ The word it in paragraph 3 refers to …</p>\n<p>▸ It can be inferred from paragraph 4 that …</p>\n<p>▸ All of the following are mentioned EXCEPT …</p>\n<p>▸ Which of the following best restates …?</p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><strong><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المهارة</span></strong></p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">في اختبار STEP الحقيقي يتبع كل نص مجموعة متنوعة من الأسئلة: الفكرة الرئيسة، والتفاصيل، والمفردات، والإحالة، والاستنتاج، والغرض، وNOT/EXCEPT، وإعادة الصياغة، فيجب أن تتعرف على نوع كل سؤال بسرعة وتغيّر الاستراتيجية. ولا تتوقف عند كل كلمة صعبة، بل امسح النص في دقيقة تقريبًا ثم أجب مع الرجوع إلى المكان الدقيق، وتذكر أن الأسئلة غالبًا تتبع ترتيب النص.</span></p>\n<h3>Strategy</h3>\n<blockquote>\n<p>1. Skim the passage in about one minute: read the first sentence of each paragraph.</p>\n<p>2. For each question, first identify its TYPE (detail, vocabulary, reference, inference …).</p>\n<p>3. Use the line or paragraph number to go straight to the right place.</p>\n<p>4. Apply the strategy for that type (key words, context clues, look back, confirm three true options …).</p>\n<p>5. Answer whole-passage questions after the detailed ones, when you understand the text better.</p>\n<p>6. Never leave a question blank — there is no penalty for guessing.</p>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">امسح النص في دقيقة، ثم حدد نوع كل سؤال، واذهب مباشرة إلى الفقرة المذكورة، وطبّق الاستراتيجية المناسبة، وأجب عن أسئلة النص الكامل بعد الأسئلة التفصيلية، ولا تترك سؤالًا بلا إجابة.</span></p>\n</blockquote>\n<h3>Traps and Distractor Types</h3>\n<blockquote>\n<p>✗ Using the same strategy for every question type.</p>\n<p>✗ Reading the whole passage slowly before looking at the questions.</p>\n<p>✗ Spending too long on one difficult vocabulary item.</p>\n<p>✗ Answering a main-idea question with the idea of the first paragraph only.</p>\n<p>✗ Forgetting that a question is negative (NOT / EXCEPT).</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h3>Worked Example (not counted in the quiz)</h3>\n<p><strong>Example text: Glass Frogs</strong></p>\n<p><em>(65 words)</em></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>[1]</strong> Glass frogs, found in the rainforests of Central and South America, have such transparent skin that their internal organs can be seen from below. Recently, scientists discovered how they become even more invisible while sleeping: they store almost all their red blood cells in the liver, which is covered by a mirror-like layer. This makes them nearly impossible for predators to see on green leaves.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Example 1. The word transparent is closest in meaning to …</strong></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>A. colourful B. clear enough to see through</p>\n<p>C. very thick D. poisonous</p>\n<p><strong>Think:</strong> Type: vocabulary. Clue: internal organs can be seen.</p>\n<p><strong>Think:</strong> The answer is B.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Example 2. It can be inferred that red blood cells …</strong></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>A. would make the frogs more visible if they stayed in the blood</p>\n<p>B. are produced only at night</p>\n<p>C. are dangerous to predators</p>\n<p>D. are stored in the skin</p>\n<p><strong>Think:</strong> Type: inference. Storing them in the liver makes the frog more invisible.</p>\n<p><strong>Think:</strong> So in the blood they would be visible.</p>\n<p><strong>Think:</strong> The answer is A.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p class=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\"><span dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">حدد نوع السؤال أولًا: الأول مفردات بقرينة السياق، والثاني استنتاج من سبب إخفاء خلايا الدم.</span></p>","wu":"Worked example: Example 1 B · Example 2 A","quiz":[{"l":["Read each text and answer the questions. Choose the best answer (A, B, C or D).","اقرأ كل نص ثم أجب عن الأسئلة التي تليه، واختر الإجابة الصحيحة من بين البدائل الأربعة.","Text 1 (Questions 1–10): Photosynthesis and the global food supply","(331 words)","[1] Almost every meal on Earth begins with photosynthesis, the process by which green plants, algae and some bacteria use sunlight to make food. Inside the leaves of plants, tiny structures called chloroplasts contain a green pigment, chlorophyll, which absorbs light energy. The plant uses this energy to combine carbon dioxide from the air with water from the soil, producing glucose, a type of sugar, and releasing oxygen as a by-product. Almost all of the oxygen in the air we breathe was produced in this way, over billions of years.","[2] Glucose is the plant's fuel. Some of it is used immediately for energy, while the rest is turned into starch, cellulose and other substances that the plant needs to grow. When we eat rice, wheat or potatoes, we are mainly eating starch made through photosynthesis. Even meat and milk ultimately depend on it, because the animals that provide them eat plants.","[3] Despite its importance, photosynthesis is surprisingly inefficient. Most crops convert less than two percent of the sunlight that falls on them into stored energy. One reason is an enzyme called RuBisCO, which is responsible for capturing carbon dioxide. It works slowly and sometimes grabs oxygen by mistake, a process that wastes energy. In hot, dry conditions, this mistake becomes more frequent, which is one reason why yields fall during heat waves.","[4] With the world's population expected to approach ten billion by 2050, scientists are searching for ways to make photosynthesis more efficient. Some researchers have modified tobacco plants so that they recover faster when moving from bright sunlight into shade; in field trials, these plants grew up to twenty percent larger. Others are trying to transfer more efficient forms of photosynthesis, found in crops such as maize and sugar cane, into rice and wheat.","[5] These projects face scientific, economic and regulatory challenges, and it may take decades before improved crops reach farmers' fields. Nevertheless, many scientists believe that redesigning photosynthesis could become one of the most important achievements of this century."],"q":[{"n":1,"s":"What is the main idea of the passage?","o":["Chlorophyll is green because it absorbs light.","Tobacco plants grow larger in the shade.","Meat and milk are healthier than rice.","Photosynthesis is vital for food, but scientists are trying to make it more efficient."],"k":3,"e":"[main idea] Importance → inefficiency → research.","h":"الفكرة الرئيسة."},{"n":2,"s":"The phrase by-product in paragraph 1 is closest in meaning to …","o":["the most important product","a harmful chemical","something produced in addition to the main product","a type of sugar"],"k":2,"e":"[vocabulary in context] producing glucose … releasing oxygen as a by-product.","h":"منتج ثانوي."},{"n":3,"s":"According to paragraph 1, which of the following is NOT used by plants in photosynthesis?","o":["sunlight","carbon dioxide","nitrogen from the soil","water"],"k":2,"e":"[NOT / EXCEPT] light ✓ CO2 ✓ water ✓; nitrogen ✗.","h":"غير مذكور."},{"n":4,"s":"Why does the author say that even meat and milk depend on photosynthesis?","o":["Animals perform photosynthesis.","Meat contains chlorophyll.","Milk is made from starch.","The animals that produce them eat plants."],"k":3,"e":"[cause–effect] because the animals that provide them eat plants.","h":"السبب."},{"n":5,"s":"The word It in paragraph 3 refers to …","o":["sunlight","RuBisCO","carbon dioxide","stored energy"],"k":1,"e":"[reference] an enzyme called RuBisCO … It works slowly.","h":"الإنزيم."},{"n":6,"s":"Which of the following best restates the sentence In hot, dry conditions, this mistake becomes more frequent, which is one reason why yields fall during heat waves?","o":["Heat waves stop the enzyme from making mistakes.","Yields always rise in hot, dry weather.","Heat and dryness make the enzyme's error happen more often, partly explaining lower harvests in heat waves.","The enzyme only works during heat waves."],"k":2,"e":"[paraphrase] more frequent mistake + partial reason for falling yields.","h":"الصياغة الصحيحة."},{"n":7,"s":"It can be inferred from paragraph 3 that improving RuBisCO could …","o":["make plants turn red","increase the amount of food that crops produce","stop plants needing water","reduce oxygen in the air"],"k":1,"e":"[inference] inefficiency caused by RuBisCO → improving it would raise yields.","h":"زيادة المحصول."},{"n":8,"s":"Why does the author mention the modified tobacco plants?","o":["to warn about smoking","to show that tobacco is a food crop","to give an example of successful research","to explain how chlorophyll works"],"k":2,"e":"[purpose] these plants grew up to twenty percent larger.","h":"مثال على النجاح."},{"n":9,"s":"The word regulatory in paragraph 5 is related to …","o":["official rules and laws","the weather","the size of farms","the speed of growth"],"k":0,"e":"[vocabulary in context] scientific, economic and regulatory challenges.","h":"تنظيمي (قانوني)."},{"n":10,"s":"The author's attitude towards efforts to redesign photosynthesis is best described as …","o":["completely negative","hopeful but realistic about the difficulties","uninterested","certain of immediate success"],"k":1,"e":"[attitude / tone] challenges … decades … Nevertheless … most important achievements.","h":"متفائل بواقعية."}]},{"l":["Text 2 (Questions 11–20): Bilingualism and the brain","(336 words)","[1] For much of the twentieth century, many parents and teachers believed that learning two languages in early childhood would confuse children and slow their development. Some immigrant families were even advised to stop speaking their home language. Research since the 1960s has largely overturned this view, revealing that bilingualism can have measurable benefits for the brain. Today, more than half of the world's population is thought to use two or more languages in daily life, so these findings affect a very large number of people.","[2] One of the most consistent findings concerns executive function, the set of mental skills that help us plan, focus attention and switch between tasks. Because both languages remain active in a bilingual person's mind, the brain must constantly select one and suppress the other. This daily practice appears to strengthen the brain's control system. In several experiments, bilingual children performed better than monolingual children on tasks that required them to ignore distracting information.","[3] Some studies have also suggested benefits later in life. Research in Canada and India found that bilingual patients developed symptoms of dementia, on average, four to five years later than monolingual patients with similar levels of brain damage. Scientists describe this effect as cognitive reserve: a mind that has been exercised throughout life can cope with damage for longer.","[4] However, the picture is not entirely simple. Some recent large studies have failed to find a clear bilingual advantage in executive function, and researchers continue to debate how strong the effect really is and whether it depends on how often and how early both languages are used. What is clear is that bilingualism does not cause harm. Children who learn two languages may know fewer words in each language at first, but their total vocabulary across both is usually similar to that of monolingual children, and they soon catch up.","[5] For Arabic-speaking families raising children who also learn English, the message is encouraging: maintaining the home language while learning a second one is not a burden but an investment."],"q":[{"n":11,"s":"Which is the best title for the passage?","o":["How to Learn English Quickly","Bilingualism and the Brain: Benefits and Debates","The History of Immigration","Why Children Forget Words"],"k":1,"e":"[best title] Covers old beliefs, benefits, debate and conclusion.","h":"العنوان."},{"n":12,"s":"The word overturned in paragraph 1 is closest in meaning to …","o":["confirmed","ignored","made popular","shown to be wrong"],"k":3,"e":"[vocabulary in context] Research has largely overturned this view.","h":"قلب / أبطل."},{"n":13,"s":"According to paragraph 2, why might bilingualism strengthen the brain's control system?","o":["Bilingual people sleep more.","Bilingual children read more books.","The brain constantly selects one language and suppresses the other.","One language is always switched off."],"k":2,"e":"[cause–effect] must constantly select one and suppress the other.","h":"السبب."},{"n":14,"s":"The word suppress in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to …","o":["hold back","translate","repeat","learn"],"k":0,"e":"[vocabulary in context] select one and suppress the other.","h":"يكبح."},{"n":15,"s":"Which of the following best restates the sentence Bilingual patients developed symptoms of dementia, on average, four to five years later than monolingual patients with similar levels of brain damage?","o":["Bilingual patients never develop dementia.","Monolingual patients developed dementia four to five years later.","Bilingual patients had less brain damage, so they were healthier.","With similar brain damage, people who spoke two languages typically showed signs of dementia several years later."],"k":3,"e":"[paraphrase] similar damage, later symptoms on average.","h":"الصياغة الصحيحة."},{"n":16,"s":"The phrase this effect in paragraph 3 refers to …","o":["the later appearance of dementia symptoms in bilingual people","the brain damage itself","research in India","the switching between tasks"],"k":0,"e":"[reference] developed symptoms later … Scientists describe this effect as cognitive reserve.","h":"التأخر."},{"n":17,"s":"What is the function of paragraph 4?","o":["to give more examples of benefits","to describe how to teach two languages","to show that the research findings are not completely agreed upon","to criticize immigrant families"],"k":2,"e":"[insertion / paragraph function] However, the picture is not entirely simple.","h":"وظيفة الفقرة."},{"n":18,"s":"All of the following are stated in paragraph 4 EXCEPT …","o":["some studies failed to find a clear advantage","bilingualism does not cause harm","bilingual children's total vocabulary is usually similar","bilingual children always know more words in each language"],"k":3,"e":"[NOT / EXCEPT] They may know FEWER words in each language at first.","h":"خطأ."},{"n":19,"s":"It can be inferred that the advice given to some immigrant families in the past was …","o":["supported by modern research","based on a mistaken belief","good for executive function","given by bilingual teachers"],"k":1,"e":"[inference] advised to stop speaking their home language … view overturned.","h":"مبني على خطأ."},{"n":20,"s":"The author would most likely advise Arabic-speaking parents to …","o":["stop speaking Arabic at home","delay English until secondary school","keep using Arabic at home while their children learn English","teach only one language"],"k":2,"e":"[inference] maintaining the home language … is an investment.","h":"النصيحة."}]},{"l":["Text 3 (Questions 21–30): How Arabic numerals reached Europe","(331 words)","[1] The numbers we use every day — 0, 1, 2, 3 and so on — are often called Arabic numerals in English. In fact, their history is a story of knowledge passed between civilizations. The system originated in India, where mathematicians had developed a method of writing any number with just ten symbols, using the position of each digit to show its value. Crucially, it included a symbol for zero. Without zero, it is difficult to tell the difference between numbers such as 25, 205 and 250.","[2] Scholars in the Islamic world learned this system in the eighth and ninth centuries, especially in Baghdad, which was then a great centre of learning. The Persian mathematician Muhammad ibn Musa al-Khwarizmi wrote a book explaining how to calculate with the Indian numerals around the year 825. His name, translated into Latin as Algoritmi, gave us the modern word algorithm, and another of his works gave us the word algebra.","[3] Europe was slow to adopt the new system. At the time, Europeans used Roman numerals, such as XII for twelve and MCM for 1900. These were acceptable for recording numbers but very awkward for calculation; multiplying two large Roman numbers was a slow and difficult task, usually done with a counting board. The new numerals reached Europe partly through Muslim Spain, where Arabic books were translated into Latin.","[4] The decisive step came in 1202, when the Italian mathematician Leonardo of Pisa, known as Fibonacci, published Liber Abaci. Having grown up in North Africa, where his father worked as a trade official, he had studied mathematics with Arab teachers. His book showed merchants how the new numerals made accounting and calculation much easier.","[5] Even so, resistance continued. In 1299, the city of Florence banned the use of the new numerals in official records, partly because they were easier to alter than Roman ones. It was not until printing spread in the fifteenth and sixteenth centuries that the system finally became standard across Europe — and, eventually, the world."],"q":[{"n":21,"s":"What is the main idea of the passage?","o":["Roman numerals are easier than Arabic numerals.","The numerals we use today travelled from India through the Islamic world to Europe.","Fibonacci invented the number zero.","Florence was the first city to use Arabic numerals."],"k":1,"e":"[main idea] origin India → Baghdad → Europe.","h":"الفكرة الرئيسة."},{"n":22,"s":"The word Crucially in paragraph 1 suggests that the symbol for zero was …","o":["extremely important","a small detail","a mistake","a later addition"],"k":0,"e":"[attitude / tone] Crucially, it included a symbol for zero.","h":"بالغ الأهمية."},{"n":23,"s":"According to paragraph 2, the word algorithm comes from …","o":["the city of Baghdad","an Indian number","the Latin word for zero","al-Khwarizmi's name"],"k":3,"e":"[detail] His name, translated as Algoritmi, gave us … algorithm.","h":"أصل الكلمة."},{"n":24,"s":"The word His in paragraph 2 refers to …","o":["Baghdad's","an Indian mathematician's","a European's","al-Khwarizmi's"],"k":3,"e":"[reference] al-Khwarizmi wrote a book … His name …","h":"الخوارزمي."},{"n":25,"s":"Why were Roman numerals awkward for calculation?","o":["They had no symbol for one.","Multiplying large Roman numbers was slow and difficult.","They were written in Arabic.","They could only show small numbers."],"k":1,"e":"[cause–effect] multiplying two large Roman numbers was a slow and difficult task.","h":"صعوبة الحساب."},{"n":26,"s":"The word awkward in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to …","o":["difficult to use","beautiful","rare","modern"],"k":0,"e":"[vocabulary in context] acceptable for recording but very awkward for calculation.","h":"غير عملي."},{"n":27,"s":"It can be inferred that Fibonacci learned about the new numerals because …","o":["he grew up in North Africa and studied with Arab teachers","he lived in India","he read Roman books","he worked in Florence"],"k":0,"e":"[inference] Having grown up in North Africa … studied with Arab teachers.","h":"سبب معرفته."},{"n":28,"s":"Which of the following is NOT mentioned as a way the numerals reached or spread in Europe?","o":["through translations in Muslim Spain","through Fibonacci's book","through trade with China","through the spread of printing"],"k":2,"e":"[NOT / EXCEPT] Spain ✓ Fibonacci ✓ printing ✓; China ✗.","h":"غير مذكور."},{"n":29,"s":"Why did Florence ban the new numerals in 1299?","o":["They were easier to change than Roman numerals.","They were too difficult to learn.","Fibonacci was not popular there.","They had no symbol for zero."],"k":0,"e":"[cause–effect] partly because they were easier to alter than Roman ones.","h":"سبب المنع."},{"n":30,"s":"How is the passage mainly organized?","o":["as a comparison of two mathematicians","as a problem and its solution","as a list of opinions","in chronological order, following the history of the numerals"],"k":3,"e":"[organization] 8th–9th c. → 825 → 1202 → 1299 → 15th–16th c.","h":"ترتيب زمني."}]}],"n":30}],"sc":{},"review":{"quiz":[{"l":["Unit Review — 50 questions on new texts. Read each text and choose the best answer (A, B, C or D).","مراجعة الوحدة: خمسون سؤالًا على نصوص جديدة، اقرأ كل نص واختر الإجابة الصحيحة.","Text 1 (Questions 1–10): The Green Riyadh initiative","(334 words)","[1] Riyadh is one of the fastest-growing capitals in the world, but for decades it has also been one of the least green. Studies carried out before 2019 found that the city had less than two square metres of green space per person, far below the levels recommended by international organizations. In a city where summer temperatures regularly exceed 45°C, the lack of trees made streets uncomfortably hot and discouraged people from walking. Many residents simply moved from one air-conditioned space to another by car, even for very short distances.","[2] Green Riyadh, launched in 2019 as one of the capital's major projects, aims to change this dramatically. Its plan is to plant around 7.5 million trees across the city by 2030, in parks, along roads, in school grounds, around mosques and in residential neighbourhoods. Officials say the project will increase green space per person to around twenty-eight square metres and could reduce temperatures in some areas by up to two degrees.","[3] The choice of trees is crucial. Planners have selected mainly native and drought-tolerant species, such as ghaf, sidr and acacia, which need far less water than the lawns and palm trees that were often planted in the past. Most of the trees are irrigated with treated wastewater rather than with precious drinking water, and a modern drip system delivers water directly to the roots, reducing waste.","[4] Supporters argue that the benefits extend well beyond cooler streets. Trees filter dust and pollutants from the air, absorb carbon dioxide and provide habitats for birds. Research from other cities suggests that green neighbourhoods also encourage physical activity and may reduce stress.","[5] Nevertheless, the project faces real challenges. Young trees require years of careful maintenance before they can survive on their own, and many urban tree-planting campaigns in other countries have failed because trees were planted and then neglected. The true test of Green Riyadh, therefore, will not be how many trees are planted, but how many are still standing — and thriving — twenty years from now."],"q":[{"n":1,"s":"What is the main idea of the passage?","o":["Riyadh is the hottest capital in the world.","Green Riyadh aims to make the city greener and cooler, but its long-term success depends on care.","Ghaf trees need very little water.","Tree-planting campaigns always fail."],"k":1,"e":"[main idea · 4.6] Problem, plan, tree choice, benefits, challenge.","h":"الفكرة الرئيسة."},{"n":2,"s":"It can be inferred from paragraph 1 that before 2019 …","o":["Riyadh had more parks than most capitals","international organizations ignored Riyadh","summer temperatures were lower than today","few people chose to walk in Riyadh's streets in summer"],"k":3,"e":"[inference · 4.1] lack of trees … discouraged people from walking.","h":"استنتاج."},{"n":3,"s":"Which of the following best restates the sentence Officials say the project will increase green space per person to around twenty-eight square metres and could reduce temperatures in some areas by up to two degrees?","o":["Officials have proved that every area will be two degrees cooler.","The project will reduce green space to twenty-eight square metres.","According to officials, the project should greatly increase green space and may lower temperatures slightly in certain areas.","Officials say temperatures will rise by two degrees."],"k":2,"e":"[paraphrase · 4.3] will increase … could reduce in some areas.","h":"الصياغة."},{"n":4,"s":"All of the following are mentioned as places where trees will be planted EXCEPT …","o":["along roads","in school grounds","on rooftops of office towers","around mosques"],"k":2,"e":"[NOT / EXCEPT · 4.4] roads ✓ schools ✓ mosques ✓; rooftops ✗.","h":"غير مذكور."},{"n":5,"s":"Why have planners chosen ghaf, sidr and acacia?","o":["They are native and need little water.","They grow the fastest of all trees.","They produce fruit for sale.","They were planted in the past."],"k":0,"e":"[cause–effect · 4.6] native and drought-tolerant species … need far less water.","h":"السبب."},{"n":6,"s":"The word precious in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to …","o":["dirty","cheap","frozen","valuable and limited"],"k":3,"e":"[vocabulary in context · 4.6] rather than with precious drinking water.","h":"ثمين."},{"n":7,"s":"Which of the following is NOT mentioned as a benefit of trees in paragraph 4?","o":["filtering dust","producing building materials","absorbing carbon dioxide","providing habitats for birds"],"k":1,"e":"[NOT / EXCEPT · 4.4] dust ✓ CO2 ✓ habitats ✓; building materials ✗.","h":"غير مذكور."},{"n":8,"s":"How is the passage mainly organized?","o":["A problem is presented, followed by a plan, its benefits and its challenges.","Two cities are compared.","Events are told in time order from 1900.","A scientific experiment is described."],"k":0,"e":"[organization · 4.5] lack of green → plan → benefits → challenges.","h":"التنظيم."},{"n":9,"s":"The author's attitude towards Green Riyadh is best described as …","o":["strongly opposed","completely indifferent","supportive but cautious","uncritically enthusiastic"],"k":2,"e":"[attitude / tone · 4.2] presents benefits, then Nevertheless … true test.","h":"داعم بحذر."},{"n":10,"s":"It can be inferred that the author believes the most important measure of success is …","o":["the number of trees planted by 2030","the cost of the project","the number of species chosen","the long-term survival of the trees"],"k":3,"e":"[inference · 4.1] not how many trees are planted, but how many are still standing.","h":"مقياس النجاح."}]},{"l":["Text 2 (Questions 11–20): The science of happiness","(333 words)","[1] For most of the twentieth century, psychology focused on problems: depression, anxiety and other forms of mental illness. In the late 1990s, a group of researchers led by Martin Seligman argued that the field should also study what makes life go well. The result was positive psychology, a branch of science that examines happiness, meaning and wellbeing in a systematic way. Since then, thousands of studies have been published, and courses on the science of happiness have become some of the most popular at universities around the world.","[2] One of its most striking findings is that money matters less than many people expect. Income does increase happiness for people who cannot afford basic needs, but once those needs are met, additional wealth brings smaller and smaller gains. People also adapt quickly to improvements in their circumstances: the excitement of a new car or a larger house tends to fade within months, a phenomenon psychologists call the hedonic treadmill.","[3] What, then, does make people happier? Decades of research point consistently to relationships. A Harvard study that has followed hundreds of men since 1938 found that the quality of their close relationships at age fifty predicted their health and happiness at eighty better than their cholesterol levels did. Other factors include having a sense of purpose, helping others, spending time in nature and getting enough sleep.","[4] Interestingly, how people spend money also matters. Experiments show that spending on experiences, such as trips or meals with friends, generally produces more lasting happiness than spending on objects. Spending money on other people, even small amounts, tends to make the giver happier than spending the same amount on themselves.","[5] Critics warn that positive psychology is sometimes oversimplified in popular books and social media, where happiness is presented as something people can achieve simply by thinking positive thoughts. Serious researchers stress that sadness and anger are normal parts of life and that wellbeing also depends on circumstances, such as health, safety and fair treatment, which individuals cannot always control."],"q":[{"n":11,"s":"Which is the best title for the passage?","o":["The Life of Martin Seligman","What Science Tells Us About Happiness","How to Buy a New Car","Mental Illness in the Twentieth Century"],"k":1,"e":"[best title · 4.6] The passage reviews research on happiness.","h":"العنوان."},{"n":12,"s":"It can be inferred from paragraph 1 that, before the late 1990s, psychology …","o":["paid little attention to what makes life good","was not considered a science","focused mainly on happiness","did not study depression"],"k":0,"e":"[inference · 4.1] focused on problems … should also study what makes life go well.","h":"استنتاج."},{"n":13,"s":"Which of the following best restates the sentence Once those needs are met, additional wealth brings smaller and smaller gains?","o":["More money always doubles happiness.","People with basic needs met become unhappy.","After basic needs are satisfied, more money adds less and less happiness.","Wealth has no effect on anyone's happiness."],"k":2,"e":"[paraphrase · 4.3] after needs met → diminishing gains.","h":"الصياغة."},{"n":14,"s":"The phrase hedonic treadmill in paragraph 2 refers to …","o":["people quickly getting used to improvements so that happiness returns to normal","a type of exercise machine","the excitement of buying a car","a mental illness"],"k":0,"e":"[reference · 4.6] excitement … tends to fade within months, a phenomenon …","h":"التكيّف."},{"n":15,"s":"Why does the author mention the Harvard study?","o":["to show that cholesterol is unimportant","to describe life in 1938","to criticize Harvard","to support the claim that relationships are central to happiness"],"k":3,"e":"[purpose · 4.6] quality of close relationships … predicted health and happiness.","h":"دعم الادعاء."},{"n":16,"s":"All of the following are mentioned as factors that make people happier EXCEPT …","o":["having a sense of purpose","having a large house","helping others","spending time in nature"],"k":1,"e":"[NOT / EXCEPT · 4.4] purpose ✓ helping ✓ nature ✓ sleep ✓; large house is used as a fading example.","h":"ليس عاملًا."},{"n":17,"s":"Which of the following best restates the sentence Spending money on other people, even small amounts, tends to make the giver happier than spending the same amount on themselves?","o":["Giving large amounts makes people unhappy.","People usually feel happier when they spend money on others rather than on themselves, even if the amount is small.","People are happiest when they spend only on themselves.","Only large gifts increase happiness."],"k":1,"e":"[paraphrase · 4.3] on others > on self, even small amounts, tends to.","h":"الصياغة."},{"n":18,"s":"Paragraph 4 is mainly developed by …","o":["describing the history of money","listing types of mental illness","telling a personal story","contrasting two ways of spending money"],"k":3,"e":"[organization · 4.5] experiences vs objects; others vs self.","h":"مقارنة."},{"n":19,"s":"The author's attitude towards the way positive psychology appears in popular books is …","o":["admiring","neutral","critical","enthusiastic"],"k":2,"e":"[attitude / tone · 4.2] Critics warn … oversimplified; serious researchers stress …","h":"ناقد."},{"n":20,"s":"It can be inferred from paragraph 5 that serious researchers would disagree that …","o":["relationships matter","sleep affects wellbeing","anyone can be happy just by thinking positively","sadness is normal"],"k":2,"e":"[inference · 4.1] happiness presented as achievable simply by thinking positive thoughts.","h":"استنتاج."}]},{"l":["Text 3 (Questions 21–30): Fast fashion and the environment","(325 words)","[1] Clothes have never been cheaper or more plentiful. Over the past two decades, global clothing production has roughly doubled, while the average number of times each item is worn before being thrown away has fallen sharply. This trend, known as fast fashion, is driven by brands that copy catwalk designs and deliver new collections to shops every few weeks, at prices low enough to encourage impulse buying. Online shopping and social media advertising have accelerated the trend even further.","[2] For consumers, the attractions are obvious: fashionable clothes at very low prices. The environmental costs, however, are much less visible. The fashion industry is estimated to produce several percent of global greenhouse-gas emissions — more than international flights and shipping combined, according to some estimates. Producing a single cotton T-shirt can require around 2,700 litres of water, enough for one person to drink for more than two years.","[3] Synthetic fabrics create another problem. Around sixty percent of clothing is now made from plastics such as polyester and nylon. Every time these clothes are washed, they release tiny plastic fibres, which pass through water-treatment plants and enter rivers and oceans. Scientists have found such microfibres in fish, sea salt and even drinking water.","[4] Waste is the final stage of the cycle. In many countries, the majority of discarded clothes end up in landfill sites or are burned; less than one percent of the material is recycled into new clothing. Much of the clothing donated in wealthy countries is shipped abroad, where large quantities cannot be resold and are eventually dumped.","[5] Some brands have begun to respond with recycling schemes, repair services and collections made from recycled fibres. Critics, however, argue that these efforts are small compared with the scale of production and that the most effective solution is simply to buy fewer clothes and wear them longer. Some young people are also turning to second-hand shops and clothing swaps, which suggests that attitudes may slowly be changing."],"q":[{"n":21,"s":"What is the main idea of the passage?","o":["Cotton T-shirts need a lot of water.","Fast fashion offers cheap clothes but causes serious environmental damage.","Brands copy catwalk designs.","Second-hand shops are becoming popular."],"k":1,"e":"[main idea · 4.6] Overview: cheap clothes vs environmental costs.","h":"الفكرة الرئيسة."},{"n":22,"s":"It can be inferred from paragraph 1 that fast-fashion brands want customers to …","o":["keep clothes for many years","repair their old clothes","buy only expensive items","buy clothes often and without much thought"],"k":3,"e":"[inference · 4.1] new collections every few weeks … impulse buying.","h":"الشراء المتكرر."},{"n":23,"s":"The word impulse in paragraph 1 is closest in meaning to …","o":["careful","sudden, unplanned","expensive","online"],"k":1,"e":"[vocabulary in context · 4.6] prices low enough to encourage impulse buying.","h":"اندفاعي."},{"n":24,"s":"Which of the following best restates the sentence The environmental costs, however, are much less visible?","o":["The environment benefits from fast fashion.","Everyone can easily see the environmental costs.","But the damage to the environment is far harder to notice.","Fast fashion has no environmental costs."],"k":2,"e":"[paraphrase · 4.3] however + much less visible.","h":"الصياغة."},{"n":25,"s":"Why does the author compare the water for one T-shirt with drinking water for one person?","o":["to make the large amount of water easier to imagine","to show that cotton is healthy","to recommend drinking more water","to compare two kinds of shirts"],"k":0,"e":"[purpose · 4.6] enough for one person to drink for more than two years.","h":"التوضيح."},{"n":26,"s":"According to paragraph 3, all of the following contain microfibres EXCEPT …","o":["cotton plants","fish","sea salt","drinking water"],"k":0,"e":"[NOT / EXCEPT · 4.4] fish ✓ salt ✓ water ✓; cotton plants ✗.","h":"غير مذكور."},{"n":27,"s":"Which of the following best restates the sentence Less than one percent of the material is recycled into new clothing?","o":["Most old clothes are recycled into new ones.","Almost none of the material from old clothes is made into new clothes.","One percent of clothes are burned.","All donated clothes are recycled."],"k":1,"e":"[paraphrase · 4.3] less than one percent = almost none.","h":"الصياغة."},{"n":28,"s":"How is the passage mainly organized?","o":["It compares two clothing brands.","It describes the history of fashion.","It follows the life cycle of clothing from production to waste and then discusses responses.","It lists the opinions of shoppers."],"k":2,"e":"[organization · 4.5] production → washing → waste → responses.","h":"التنظيم."},{"n":29,"s":"Which of the following is NOT mentioned as a response by brands?","o":["banning synthetic fabrics","recycling schemes","repair services","collections made from recycled fibres"],"k":0,"e":"[NOT / EXCEPT · 4.4] No ban mentioned.","h":"غير مذكور."},{"n":30,"s":"The author's attitude towards the change in young people's habits is …","o":["angry","dismissive","completely certain","cautiously hopeful"],"k":3,"e":"[attitude / tone · 4.2] suggests that attitudes may slowly be changing.","h":"متفائل بحذر."}]},{"l":["Text 4 (Questions 31–40): Deep-sea exploration","(336 words)","[1] It is often said that we know more about the surface of the Moon than about the floor of our own oceans. Although oceans cover about seventy-one percent of the Earth's surface, only around a quarter of the sea floor has been mapped in high detail, and a tiny fraction has ever been seen directly by humans. The deep sea — usually defined as waters below 200 metres — remains the largest and least explored environment on the planet.","[2] Exploring it is extremely difficult. Sunlight disappears completely below about 1,000 metres, temperatures are close to freezing, and the pressure increases by roughly one atmosphere every ten metres. At the bottom of the Mariana Trench, nearly 11,000 metres deep, the pressure is more than a thousand times greater than at the surface, enough to crush an ordinary submarine.","[3] Only a handful of people have reached the deepest point. The first were Jacques Piccard and Don Walsh in 1960, in a vessel called the Trieste. Today, most exploration is done by remotely operated vehicles and autonomous robots, which can stay underwater for long periods without risking human lives. Equipped with cameras, lights and robotic arms, they collect samples and send back images of a world few people will ever visit.","[4] What they have found has surprised scientists. Far from being empty, the deep sea is home to a remarkable variety of life, from glowing fish and giant squid to communities of animals that live around hydrothermal vents — cracks in the sea floor that release hot, mineral-rich water. These communities do not depend on sunlight at all; instead, bacteria obtain energy from chemicals in the water, forming the base of a unique food chain.","[5] The deep sea is now attracting commercial interest, especially for minerals used in batteries. Many scientists, however, urge caution, arguing that mining could destroy ecosystems we have barely begun to understand. The Red Sea itself contains deep, hot, salty pools that researchers, including teams from KAUST, are studying for clues about life in extreme environments."],"q":[{"n":31,"s":"Which is the best title for the passage?","o":["How Submarines Are Built","The Surface of the Moon","The Deep Sea: Earth's Last Great Frontier","Battery Minerals"],"k":2,"e":"[best title · 4.6] Covers how little is known, difficulty, exploration, discoveries, future.","h":"العنوان."},{"n":32,"s":"Which of the following best restates the sentence It is often said that we know more about the surface of the Moon than about the floor of our own oceans?","o":["We know nothing about the Moon.","The ocean floor has been completely mapped.","People often claim that the ocean floor is less known to us than the Moon's surface.","The Moon is closer than the ocean floor."],"k":2,"e":"[paraphrase · 4.3] more about Moon than ocean floor.","h":"الصياغة."},{"n":33,"s":"All of the following are mentioned as challenges of deep-sea exploration EXCEPT …","o":["complete darkness","strong winds","near-freezing temperatures","enormous pressure"],"k":1,"e":"[NOT / EXCEPT · 4.4] darkness ✓ cold ✓ pressure ✓; wind ✗.","h":"ليس تحديًا مذكورًا."},{"n":34,"s":"It can be inferred from paragraph 2 that a vessel reaching the Mariana Trench must be …","o":["built to withstand extreme pressure","very light and fast","powered by sunlight","able to fly"],"k":0,"e":"[inference · 4.1] pressure … enough to crush an ordinary submarine.","h":"استنتاج."},{"n":35,"s":"Why is most exploration now done by robots?","o":["They are cheaper than cameras.","Humans are not allowed in the ocean.","Robots can see without lights.","They can stay underwater for long without risking human lives."],"k":3,"e":"[cause–effect · 4.6] can stay underwater for long periods without risking human lives.","h":"السبب."},{"n":36,"s":"The phrase Far from being empty in paragraph 4 suggests that scientists had expected the deep sea to …","o":["be full of fish","be very warm","be easy to explore","contain little life"],"k":3,"e":"[inference · 4.1] has surprised scientists … far from being empty.","h":"توقع قلة الحياة."},{"n":37,"s":"Which of the following best restates the sentence These communities do not depend on sunlight at all?","o":["The communities need a lot of sunlight.","Sunlight reaches the vents every day.","The animals around the vents survive without any energy from the sun.","The animals depend on sunlight only partly."],"k":2,"e":"[paraphrase · 4.3] do not depend … at all = without any.","h":"الصياغة."},{"n":38,"s":"Paragraph 4 is mainly developed by …","o":["describing examples of surprising deep-sea life","comparing two submarines","explaining a problem and its solution","giving the history of mining"],"k":0,"e":"[organization · 4.5] glowing fish, giant squid, vent communities.","h":"أمثلة."},{"n":39,"s":"Which of the following is NOT mentioned in connection with the Red Sea?","o":["deep, hot, salty pools","research by KAUST teams","large deposits of oil on the sea floor","clues about life in extreme environments"],"k":2,"e":"[NOT / EXCEPT · 4.4] Oil not mentioned.","h":"غير مذكور."},{"n":40,"s":"The author's attitude towards deep-sea mining is best described as …","o":["enthusiastic","concerned","indifferent","amused"],"k":1,"e":"[attitude / tone · 4.2] scientists urge caution … could destroy ecosystems.","h":"قلق."}]},{"l":["Text 5 (Questions 41–50): Ancient aflaj irrigation systems","(321 words)","[1] Long before modern pumps and pipelines, communities in the Arabian Peninsula developed ingenious ways to bring water to their fields. One of the most remarkable is the falaj (plural aflaj), a system of channels that carries water from underground sources or springs to villages and farms, using only the force of gravity. Aflaj have been used in Oman for at least 2,500 years, and similar systems existed in parts of Saudi Arabia, the UAE and Iran, where they are known as qanats.","[2] There are several types. The most impressive is the underground falaj, in which workers dug a long, gently sloping tunnel from a mother well, located near the foot of mountains where groundwater is found, to the land below. Vertical shafts were dug every few dozen metres to remove soil and provide air for the workers. Because the water travels underground, very little is lost to evaporation, a crucial advantage in one of the hottest climates on Earth.","[3] Equally impressive is the social system that manages the water. In a traditional falaj, every farmer owns shares measured in units of time rather than volume. A water master, chosen by the community, ensures that each family receives its allocated turn, which in the past was measured by the movement of the sun during the day and of the stars at night. Water first serves drinking needs, then the mosque and bathing areas, and only afterwards the fields — an order that reflects the community's priorities.","[4] In 2006, UNESCO added five Omani aflaj to its World Heritage List. However, many systems are now under pressure. Modern wells pump groundwater faster than it can be replaced, lowering the water table so that some aflaj have dried up. Others have been neglected as young people move to cities.","[5] Some engineers believe that the principles behind aflaj — using gravity, reducing evaporation and sharing water fairly — offer valuable lessons for modern water management in dry regions."],"q":[{"n":41,"s":"What is the main idea of the passage?","o":["Oman has the hottest climate on Earth.","UNESCO protects all water systems.","Modern pumps are better than aflaj.","Aflaj are ancient, clever water systems with lessons for today, but many are threatened."],"k":3,"e":"[main idea · 4.6] Definition, design, social system, threats, lessons.","h":"الفكرة الرئيسة."},{"n":42,"s":"The word ingenious in paragraph 1 is closest in meaning to …","o":["expensive","clever and original","ancient","dangerous"],"k":1,"e":"[vocabulary in context · 4.6] ingenious ways to bring water.","h":"بارع."},{"n":43,"s":"Which of the following best restates the sentence Because the water travels underground, very little is lost to evaporation?","o":["Since the water moves below the surface, hardly any of it evaporates.","Water travels underground because it evaporates.","Most of the water evaporates on its way.","Evaporation makes the water travel underground."],"k":0,"e":"[paraphrase · 4.3] cause (underground) → little evaporation.","h":"الصياغة."},{"n":44,"s":"All of the following are mentioned about underground aflaj EXCEPT …","o":["they used pumps to move the water","they started from a mother well","they had vertical shafts","they reduced evaporation"],"k":0,"e":"[NOT / EXCEPT · 4.4] gravity only — no pumps.","h":"خطأ."},{"n":45,"s":"Why were vertical shafts dug along the tunnel?","o":["to collect rainwater","to remove soil and provide air","to store food","to measure time"],"k":1,"e":"[cause–effect · 4.6] to remove soil and provide air for the workers.","h":"السبب."},{"n":46,"s":"It can be inferred from paragraph 3 that the order of water use shows that the community …","o":["did not grow crops","valued fields above drinking water","had no water master","considered human needs more important than crops"],"k":3,"e":"[inference · 4.1] drinking first, fields last → priorities.","h":"الأولويات."},{"n":47,"s":"Which of the following best restates the sentence Every farmer owns shares measured in units of time rather than volume?","o":["Farmers buy water by the litre.","Each farmer's share is the amount of time he can take water, not a fixed quantity.","Time is not important in the falaj system.","Every farmer receives the same volume of water."],"k":1,"e":"[paraphrase · 4.3] time not volume.","h":"الصياغة."},{"n":48,"s":"Which of the following is NOT given as a threat to aflaj?","o":["earthquakes destroying the tunnels","modern wells lowering the water table","neglect as young people move away","groundwater pumped too fast"],"k":0,"e":"[NOT / EXCEPT · 4.4] Earthquakes not mentioned.","h":"ليس تهديدًا مذكورًا."},{"n":49,"s":"What is the function of paragraph 4?","o":["to explain how tunnels are dug","to describe the water master","to compare Oman and Iran","to describe the recognition and the current problems of aflaj"],"k":3,"e":"[organization · 4.5] UNESCO recognition … However, under pressure.","h":"وظيفة الفقرة."},{"n":50,"s":"The author's attitude towards aflaj is best described as …","o":["critical","indifferent","sarcastic","admiring"],"k":3,"e":"[attitude / tone · 4.2] remarkable, ingenious, impressive, valuable lessons.","h":"معجب."}]}],"n":50}};
